== History ==
The Stowe gardens and estate are located close to the village of [[Stowe, Buckinghamshire|Stowe]] in [[Buckinghamshire]], England.<ref name="NT History">{{Cite web |title=History of Stowe |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/history-of-stowe |access-date=2022-07-14 |website=National Trust |archive-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715071945/https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/history-of-stowe |url-status=live }}</ref> John Temple, a wealthy wool farmer, purchased the manor and estate in 1589. Subsequent generations of Temples inherited the estate, but it was with the succession of Sir Richard Temple that the gardens began to be developed, after the completion of a new house in 1683.<ref name="NT History" />

[[Richard Temple, 1st Viscount Cobham]], inherited the estate in 1697, and in 1713 was given the title Baron Cobham. During this period, both the house and the garden were redesigned and expanded, with leading architects, designers and gardeners employed to enhance the property.<ref name="NT History" /> The installation of a variety of temples and classical features was illustrated the Temple family's wealth and status. The temples are also considered as a humorous reference to the family motto: ''TEMPLA QUAM DILECTA'' ('How beautiful are the Temples').{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=10}}

=== 1690s to 1740s ===

In the 1690s, Stowe had a modest early [[Baroque garden|Baroque]] [[parterre]] garden, but it has not survived, as it was altered and adapted as the gardens were progressively remodelled.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Jarzombek |first1=Mark M. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=HL2I_t_ZyQoC&dq=stowe+gardens+parterre&pg=PA585 |title=A Global History of Architecture |last2=Prakash |first2=Vikramaditya |date=2011-10-04 |publisher=John Wiley & Sons |isbn=978-0-470-90248-6 |page=585 |access-date=2022-09-10 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044822/https://books.google.com/books?id=HL2I_t_ZyQoC&dq=stowe+gardens+parterre&pg=PA585 |url-status=live }}</ref> Within a relatively short time, Stowe became widely renowned for its magnificent gardens created by Lord Cobham. Created in three main phases, the gardens at Stowe show the development of garden design in 18th-century England. They are also the only gardens where [[Charles Bridgeman]], [[William Kent]], and [[Capability Brown]] all made significant contributions to the character and design.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=20}}

From 1711 to 1735, Charles Bridgeman was the garden designer,<ref>{{Cite book |last=Willis |first=Peter |title=Charles Bridgeman and the English landscape garden |date=1977 |publisher=A. Zwemmer |isbn=0-302-02777-7 |location=London |page=106 |oclc=4206348 }}</ref> whilst [[John Vanbrugh]] was the architect from 1720 until his death in 1726.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Hart |first=Vaughan |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=gwfqAAAAMAAJ&q=Sir+John+Vanbrugh:+Storyteller+in+Stone,+Vaughan+Hart, |title=Sir John Vanbrugh: Storyteller in Stone |date=2008 |publisher=Yale University Press |isbn=978-0-300-11929-9 |page=210 |access-date=2022-09-10 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044827/https://books.google.com/books?id=gwfqAAAAMAAJ&q=Sir+John+Vanbrugh:+Storyteller+in+Stone,+Vaughan+Hart, |url-status=live }}</ref> They designed an [[English Baroque]] park, inspired by the work of [[George London (landscape architect)|George London]], [[Henry Wise (gardener)|Henry Wise]] and [[Stephen Switzer]]. After Vanbrugh's death in 1726, [[James Gibbs]] took over as garden architect. He also worked in the English Baroque style.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Friedman |first=Terry |title=James Gibbs |date=1984 |publisher=Published for the Paul Mellon Centre for Studies in British Art by Yale University Press |isbn=978-0-300-03172-0 |location=New Haven, Conn |page=179 |ol=2866726M }}</ref> Bridgeman was notable for the use of canalised water at Stowe.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Smith |first=Nicky |date=2006 |title=Lodge Park and Charles Bridgeman, Master of 'Incomprehensible Vastness' |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/25472343 |journal=Garden History |volume=34 |issue=2 |pages=236–248 |doi=10.2307/25472343 |jstor=25472343 |issn=0307-1243 |access-date=9 October 2022 |archive-date=9 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221009191719/https://www.jstor.org/stable/25472343 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref>

In 1731, William Kent was appointed to work with Bridgeman, whose last designs are dated 1735. After Bridgeman, Kent took over as the garden designer.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Wilson |first=Michael I. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=mXA9AAAAIAAJ |title=William Kent: Architect, Designer, Painter, Gardener, 1685-1748 |date=1984-01-01 |publisher=Routledge & Kegan Paul |isbn=978-0-7100-9983-9 |page=208 |access-date=2022-07-15 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044827/https://books.google.com/books?id=mXA9AAAAIAAJ |url-status=live }}</ref> Kent had already created the noted garden at [[Rousham House]], and he and Gibbs built temples, bridges, and other garden structures, creating a less formal style of garden.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Eyres |first=Patrick |date=1985 |title=Rousham: the Extant Augustian Garden Designed by William Kent Outside Oxford |journal=New Arcadian Journal |volume=19 |pages=1–40}}</ref> Kent's masterpiece at Stowe is the innovative Elysian Fields, which were "laid out on the latest principles of following natural lines and contours".{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=28}} With its Temple of Ancient Virtue that looks across to his Temple of British Worthies, Kent's architectural work was in the newly fashionable [[Palladian architecture|Palladian style]].{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=31}}

In March 1741, Capability Brown was appointed head gardener and he lived in the East Boycott Pavilion.{{sfn|Stroud|1984|p=52}} He had first been employed at Stowe in 1740, to support work on the water schemes on site.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Shields |first=Steffie |date=2006 |title='Mr Brown Engineer': Lancelot Brown's Early Work at Grimsthorpe Castle and Stowe |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/25472339 |journal=Garden History |volume=34 |issue=2 |pages=174–191 |doi=10.2307/25472339 |jstor=25472339 |issn=0307-1243 |access-date=2022-07-15 |archive-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715081153/https://www.jstor.org/stable/25472339 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref> Brown worked with Gibbs until 1749 and with Kent until the latter's death in 1748. Brown departed in the autumn of 1751 to start his independent career as a garden designer.{{sfn|Stroud|1984|p=55}} At that time, Bridgeman's octagonal pond and {{cvt|11|acre|adj=on}} lake were extended and given a "naturalistic" shape. A Palladian bridge was added in 1744, probably to Gibbs's design. Brown also reputedly contrived a ''Grecian Valley'' which, despite its name, was an abstract composition of landform and woodland.<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal |last=Clark |first=H. F. |date=1969 |title=The Restoration and Reclamation of Gardens |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/1586293 |journal=Occasional Paper (Garden History Society) |issue=1 |pages=3–6 |doi=10.2307/1586293 |jstor=1586293 |issn=1749-3390 |access-date=2022-07-15 |archive-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715081102/https://www.jstor.org/stable/1586293 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref> He also developed the ''Hawkwell Field'', with Gibbs's most notable building, the ''Gothic Temple'', within.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Fletcher |first=LuAnn McCracken |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=H0GIDwAAQBAJ&dq=hawkwell+field+gibbs&pg=PA147 |title=Literary Tourism and the British Isles: History, Imagination, and the Politics of Place |date=2018-12-10 |publisher=Lexington Books |isbn=978-1-4985-8124-0 |page=147 |access-date=2022-07-27 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044850/https://books.google.com/books?id=H0GIDwAAQBAJ&dq=hawkwell+field+gibbs&pg=PA147 |url-status=live }}</ref> The Temple is one of the properties leased from the National Trust by the [[Landmark Trust]], who maintain it as a holiday home.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Keay |first1=Anna |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=wkQkrgEACAAJ |title=Landmark: A History of Britain in 50 Buildings |last2=Stanford |first2=Caroline |date=2015-10-01 |publisher=Frances Lincoln Adult |isbn=978-0-7112-3645-5 |page=153 |access-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044852/https://books.google.com/books?id=wkQkrgEACAAJ |archive-date=2022-09-10 |url-status=live}}</ref> As [[John Claudius Loudon]] remarked in 1831, "nature has done little or nothing; man a great deal, and time has improved his labours".<ref>{{Cite book |last=Loudon |first=J C |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=jjuNNICK9nIC&dq=%22nature+has+done+little+or+nothing%3B+man+a+great+deal%2C+and+time+has+improved+his+labours%22&pg=PA389 |title=The Gardener's Magazine and Register of Rural and Domestic Improvement |date=1831 |publisher=Longman, Rees, Orme, Brown and Green |page=389 |access-date=2022-07-27 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044904/https://books.google.com/books?id=jjuNNICK9nIC&dq=%22nature+has+done+little+or+nothing%3B+man+a+great+deal%2C+and+time+has+improved+his+labours%22&pg=PA389 |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== 1740s to 1760s ===

[[File:Stowe - A View at the Entrance between the Pavillions.jpg|thumb|upright=1.4|Stowe, c.1750 – ''A View at the Entrance between the Pavilions'' (Victoria & Albert Museum)]]

[[Richard Grenville-Temple, 2nd Earl Temple|Earl Temple]], who had inherited Stowe from his uncle Lord Cobham, turned to a garden designer called Richard Woodward after Brown left. Woodward had worked at [[Wotton House]], the Earl's previous home.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=68}} The work of naturalising the landscape started by Brown was continued under Woodward and was accomplished by the mid-1750s.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=28}}

At the same time, Earl Temple turned his attention to the various temples and monuments. He altered several temples designed by Vanburgh and Gibbs to make them conform to his taste for [[Neoclassical architecture]]. To accomplish this, he employed [[Giovanni Battista Borra]] from July 1750 to c. 1760.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Coffin |first=David R. |date=2000 |title=Venus in the Eighteenth-Century English Garden |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/1587268 |journal=Garden History |volume=28 |issue=2 |pages=173–193 |doi=10.2307/1587268 |jstor=1587268 |issn=0307-1243 |access-date=2022-07-15 |archive-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715081155/https://www.jstor.org/stable/1587268 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref>{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=63}} Also at this time, several statues and temples were relocated within the garden, including the Fane of Pastoral Poetry.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=79}}

Earl Temple made further alterations in the gardens from the early 1760s, with alteration to both planting and structure.  Several older structures were removed, including the Witch House. Several designs for this period are attributed to his cousin [[Thomas Pitt, 1st Baron Camelford]].{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=63}} Camelford's most notable design was the Corinthian Arch.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Clarke |first=George |date=1992 |title=The Moving Temples of Stowe: Aesthetics of Change in an English Landscape over Four Generations |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/3817689 |journal=Huntington Library Quarterly |volume=55 |issue=3 |pages=501–509 |doi=10.2307/3817689 |jstor=3817689 |issn=0018-7895 |access-date=2022-07-15 |archive-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715081058/https://www.jstor.org/stable/3817689 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref>

=== 1770s To Present ===

[[File:Above Stowe Park (1978) - geograph.org.uk - 855893.jpg|thumb|upright|Aerial view south over the gardens 1978]]

Famed as a highly fashionable garden, by 1777 some visitors, such as [[Henry Temple, 2nd Viscount Palmerston]], complained that the gardens were "much behind the best modern ones in points of good taste".{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=54}}

The next owner of Stowe, the [[George Nugent-Temple-Grenville, 1st Marquess of Buckingham|Marquess of Buckingham]], made relatively few changes to the gardens, as his main contribution to the Stowe scheme was the completion of Stowe House's interior.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=142}} [[Vincenzo Valdrè]] was his architect and built a few new structures such as The Menagerie, with its formal garden and the Buckingham Lodges at the southern end of the Grand Avenue, and most notably the Queen's Temple.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=64}}

=== 19th-century Stowe ===

The last significant changes to the gardens were made by the next two owners of Stowe, the 1st and 2nd [[Duke of Buckingham|Dukes of Buckingham and Chandos]]. The former succeeded in buying the Lamport Estate in 1826, which was immediately to the east of the gardens, adding {{cvt|17|acre}} to the south-east of the gardens to form the Lamport Gardens.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=77}}

From 1840 the [[Richard Temple-Grenville, 2nd Duke of Buckingham and Chandos|2nd Duke]]'s gardener Mr Ferguson created rock structures and water features in the new Lamport Gardens. The architect [[Edward Blore]] was also employed to build the Lamport Lodge and Gates as a carriage entrance, and also remodelled the Water Stratford Lodge at the start of the Oxford Avenue.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=64}}

In 1848 the 2nd Duke was forced to sell the contents of the house and a large part of the estate in order to begin to pay off his debts. The auction by [[Christie's]] made the name of the auction house.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=12}} In 1862, [[Richard Temple-Nugent-Brydges-Chandos-Grenville, 3rd Duke of Buckingham and Chandos]] returned to Stowe and began to repair several areas of the gardens, including planting avenues of trees. In 1868 the garden was re-opened to the public.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=77-78}}

=== 20th century ===

[[File:Stowe park map ugglan.jpg|thumb|Plan of gardens, 1910]]

The remaining estate was sold in 1921 and 1922. In 1923, [[Stowe School]] was founded, which saved the house and garden from destruction.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=12}} Until 1989, the landscape garden was owned by Stowe School, who undertook some restoration work. This included the development of a restoration plan in the 1930s. The first building to be restored was the Queen's Temple, repairs to which were funded by a public appeal launched by the future [[Edward VIII]]. In the 1950s, repairs were made to the Temple of Venus, the Corinthian Arch and the Rotondo. Stowe Avenue was replanted in 1960.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=21}}
[[File:Eleven Acre Lake, Stowe - geograph.org.uk - 837805.jpg|right|thumb|The Eleven-Acre Lake]]In the 1960s, significant repairs were made to buildings such as the Lake Pavilions and the Pebble Alcove. Other works included replanting several avenues, repairs to two-thirds of the buildings, and the reclamation of six of the lakes (only the Eleven Acre Lake was not tackled). As a result of this, the school was recognised for its contribution to conservation and heritage with awards in 1974 and 1975.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=21}}

The [[National Trust]] first became significantly involved in Stowe in 1965, when John Workman was invited to compile a plan for restoration.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=24}} In 1967, 221 acres were covenanted to the National Trust and in 1985 the trust purchased Oxford Avenue, the first time it had bought land to enhance a site not under its ownership.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=22}} In 1989, much of the garden and the park was donated to the National Trust, after generous donations from the [[National Heritage Memorial Fund]] and an anonymous benefactor, which enabled an endowment for repairs to be created.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=22}} In 1993, the National Trust successfully completed an appeal for £1 million, with the aim of having the garden restored by 2000. Parallel to fund-raising, extensive garden, archaeological and biological surveys were undertaken.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=24}} Further repairs were undertaken to many monuments in the 1990s.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=25}} The ''Stowe Papers'', some 350,000 documents relating to the estate, are in the collection of the [[Huntington Library]].{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=24}}

=== 21st century ===

In 2012 the restoration of the historic New Inn was finished, providing enhanced visitor services.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Stowe you see today |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/restoring-stowe---2015-to-2019 |access-date=2022-07-15 |website=National Trust |archive-date=2016-05-13 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160513042348/http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/restoring-stowe---2015-to-2019 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2012-05-25 |title=Stowe's £9m makeover allows visitors to follow in the footsteps of 18th century tourists |website=The National Lottery Heritage Fund |url=https://www.heritagefund.org.uk/news/stowes-ps9m-makeover-allows-visitors-follow-footsteps-18th-century-tourists |access-date=2022-07-15 |archive-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715143100/https://www.heritagefund.org.uk/news/stowes-ps9m-makeover-allows-visitors-follow-footsteps-18th-century-tourists |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2015, the National Trust began a further programme of restoration, which included the recreation of the Queens Theatre, the return of many statues to former locations in the Grecian Valley, and the return of the Temple of Modern Virtue to the Elysian Fields.<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |title=Restoring Stowe |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/projects/restoring-stowe-2015-2019 |access-date=2022-07-15 |website=National Trust |archive-date=2016-06-06 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160606132910/http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/projects/restoring-stowe-2015-2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Accommodating the requirements of a 21st-century school within a historic landscape continues to create challenges. In the revised ''Buckinghamshire'', in the [[Pevsner Architectural Guides|Pevsner Buildings of England]] series published in 2003, Elizabeth Williamson wrote of areas of the garden being "disastrously invaded by school buildings".{{sfn|Williamson|Pevsner|2003|p=677}}{{efn|Despite these criticisms, the authors of the Pevsner Guide acknowledged the school's contribution: "were it not for the school, there would be no Stowe: the demolition merchants were waiting when it intervened."{{sfn|Williamson|Pevsner|2003|p=661}}}} In 2021, plans for a new Design, Technology and Engineering block in Pyramid Wood provoked controversy. The school's plans were supported by the National Trust but opposed by [[Buckinghamshire County Council]]'s own planning advisors, as well as a range of interest groups including [[The Gardens Trust]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://thegardenstrust.org/stowe-campaign-reaches-the-end-of-the-road/|title=Stowe campaign reaches the end of the road|publisher=The Gardens Trust|date=21 March 2022|access-date=22 January 2023|archive-date=21 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121184416/https://thegardenstrust.org/stowe-campaign-reaches-the-end-of-the-road/|url-status=live}}</ref> Despite objections from the council's independent advisor,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://buckinghamshire.moderngov.co.uk/mgConvert2PDF.aspx?ID=32653|title=Historic landscape advice relating to a proposed new Design Technology & Engineering (DTE) building for Stowe School|first=Matthew|last=Tickner|publisher=Buckinghamshire County Council|date=21 August 2021|access-date=22 January 2023|archive-date=21 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121184644/https://buckinghamshire.moderngov.co.uk/mgConvert2PDF.aspx?ID=32653|url-status=live}}</ref> and an appeal to the [[Secretary of State for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport]], the plans were approved in 2022.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bucks.radio/news/local-news/stowe-school-building-gets-green-light-after-appeal-against-perverse-planning-decision-fails/|title=Stowe School building gets green light after appeal against 'perverse' planning decision fails|first=Sam|last=Dean|publisher=Bucks Radio|date=25 March 2022|access-date=22 January 2023|archive-date=21 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121221257/https://www.bucks.radio/news/local-news/stowe-school-building-gets-green-light-after-appeal-against-perverse-planning-decision-fails/|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Layout ==

=== Approaching Stowe Gardens ===

In 2012, with the renovation and re-opening of the New Inn, visitors to Stowe Gardens have returned to using the historic entrance route to the site which was used by tourists in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. Most will drive between the Buckingham Lodges, before approaching the site along the Grand Avenue and turning right in front of the Corinthian Arch.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Visiting Stowe: What to expect during your visit |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/visiting-stowe-what-to-expect-during-your-visit |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=National Trust |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825123007/https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/visiting-stowe-what-to-expect-during-your-visit |url-status=live }}</ref>

Significant monuments on the route in, include:

==== The Buckingham Lodges ====

The Buckingham Lodges are 2.25 miles south-southeast of the centre of the House. Probably designed by [[Vincenzo Valdrè]] and dated 1805, they flank the southern entrance to the Grand Avenue.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=8}}

==== The Grand Avenue ====

The Grand Avenue, from [[Buckingham]] to the south and the Oxford Avenue from the south-west, which leads to the forecourt of the house. The Grand Avenue was created in the 1770s; it is {{cvt|100|ft|m}} in width and one and half miles in length, and was lined originally with [[elm]] trees. The elms succumbed in the 1970s to [[Dutch elm disease]] and were replaced with alternate [[beech]] & [[chestnut]] trees.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=165}}

====The Corinthian Arch====

Designed in 1765 by [[Thomas Pitt, 1st Baron Camelford]], Lord Temple's cousin, the arch is built from stone and is {{cvt|60|ft|m}} in height and {{cvt|60|ft|m}} wide. It is modelled on ancient Roman [[triumphal arch]]es. This is located at the northern end of the Grand Avenue 0.8 miles south-southeast of the centre of the House and is on the top of a hill. The central arch is flanked on the south side by paired [[Corinthian order|Corinthian]] [[pilaster]]s and on the north side by paired Corinthian [[engaged column]]s. The arch contains two four-storey residences. The flanking [[Tuscan order|Tuscan]] columns were added in 1780.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=9}}

==== The New Inn ====

Situated about {{cvt|100|metres|order=flip}} to the east of the Corinthian Arch, the inn was built in 1717 specifically to provide accommodation for visitors to the gardens. It was expanded and rebuilt in several phases.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=144}}  The inn housed a small brewery, a farm and dairy. It closed in the 1850s, then being used as a farm, [[Forge|smithy]] and [[kennels]] for deer hounds.<ref>{{NHLE|num=1211787|desc=New Inn Farmhouse with Outbuildings|grade=II*|access-date=26 January 2023}}</ref>

The building was purchased in a ruinous condition by the National Trust in 2005. In 2010, work started on converting it into the new [[visitor centre]]. Since 2011, this has been the entrance for visitors to the gardens.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2012-03-10 |title=New Inn Stowe visitor centre rebuild complete |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-beds-bucks-herts-17312410 |access-date=2022-08-25 |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825123006/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-beds-bucks-herts-17312410 |url-status=live }}</ref> Visitors had formerly used the Oxford Gates. The New Inn is linked by the Bell Gate Drive to the Bell Gate next to the eastern Lake Pavilion, so called because visitors used to have to ring the bell by the gate to gain admittance to the property.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=144}}

==== Ha-ha ====

The main gardens, enclosed within the [[ha-ha]]s (sunken or trenched fences) over four miles (6&nbsp;km) in length, cover over {{cvt|400|acre}}.{{sfn|Hussey|1967|p=89}}

