=== South Vista ===
[[File:Stowe House 03.jpg|right|thumb|South vista, looking north]]

The south vista includes the tree-flanked sloping lawns to the south of the House down to the Octagon Lake and a mile and a half beyond to the Corinthian Arch beyond which stretches the Grand Avenue of over a mile and a half to [[Buckingham]]. This is the oldest area of the gardens. There were walled gardens on the site of the south lawn from the 1670s that belonged to the old house. These gardens were altered in the 1680s when the house was rebuilt on the present site. They were again remodelled by Bridgeman from 1716.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=61}} The lawns with the flanking woods took on their current character from 1741 when 'Capability' Brown re-landscaped this area.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=106}}

The buildings in this area are:

==== The Doric Arch ====

[[File:Stowe Doric Arch.jpg|thumb|The Doric Arch|240x240px]]

Built of stone erected in 1768 for the visit of [[Princess Amelia of Great Britain|Princess Amelia]], probably to the design of Thomas Pitt, 1st Baron Camelford, is a simple arch flanked by fluted [[Doric order|Doric]] pilasters, with an elaborate entablature with [[triglyph]]s and carved [[Metope (architecture)|metopes]] supporting a tall attic. This leads to the Elysian fields.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=23}}

==== Apollo and the Nine Muses ====

Arranged in a semicircle near the Doric Arch there used to be statues of [[Apollo]] and the [[Muse|Nine Muses]] removed sometime after 1790.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=134}} These sculptures were created by [[John Nost]] and were originally positioned along the south vista.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Restoring Apollo and the Nine Muses to Stowe |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/projects/restoring-apollo-and-the-nine-muses-to-stowe |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=National Trust |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144618/https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/projects/restoring-apollo-and-the-nine-muses-to-stowe |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2019 the ten plinths 5 each side of the ''Doric Arch'' were recreated,{{refn|The statue of Apollo is yet to be installed.|name=first|group=lower-alpha}} and statues of the Nine Muses placed on them.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Richardson |first=Tim |date=2020-03-21 |title='The Nine Muses' recreated at Stowe after a century of archaeological detective work |work=The Telegraph |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/gardening/gardens-to-visit/nine-muses-recreated-stowe-century-ofarchaeological-detective/ |access-date=2022-08-25 |issn=0307-1235 |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144613/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/gardening/gardens-to-visit/nine-muses-recreated-stowe-century-ofarchaeological-detective/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== Statue of George II ====

[[File:King's Pillar (Statue of King George II), Stowe - Buckinghamshire, England - DSC07039.jpg|thumb|upright|The Statue of George II]]

On the western edge of the lawn, the statue was rebuilt in 2004 by the National Trust.<ref name=":6">{{Cite web |title=MNA130116 |work=National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA130116 |access-date=2022-08-25 |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144626/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA130116 |url-status=live }}</ref> This is a monument to King [[George II of Great Britain|George II]], originally built in 1724 before he became king.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=95}} The monument consists of an unfluted Corinthian column on a plinth over {{cvt|30|ft|m}} high that supports the Portland stone sculpture of the King which is a copy of the statue sold in 1921.<ref name=":6" /> The pillar has this inscription from [[Horace]]'s Ode 15, Book IV:

{{verse translation|lang=la
|Crevere Vires, Famaque & Imperi
Porrecta Majestas ad ortum
Solis ab Hesperio Cubili
Custode rerum Cæsare
GEORGIO AUGUSTO.
|Under the care of Cæsar's scepter'd hand,
With strength and fame increas'd, this favour'd Land
The Majesty of her vast Empire spread,
From the Sun rising to his Western bed.}}

=== The Elysian fields ===

[[File:Stowe Park, Buckinghamshire (4664028973).jpg|thumb|'River Styx' flows through the Elysian Fields]]

The Elysian Fields is an area to the immediate east of the South Vista; designed by William Kent, work started on this area of the gardens in 1734.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=17,86}} The area covers about {{cvt|40|acre}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=William Kent |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/profiles/william-kent---profile |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=National Trust |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144613/https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/profiles/william-kent---profile |url-status=live }}</ref> It consists of a series of buildings and monuments surrounding two narrow lakes, called the ''River [[Styx]]'', which step down to a branch of ''the Octagon Lake''.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=87}} The banks are planted with deciduous and evergreen trees.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=87}} The adoption of the name alludes to [[Elysium]], and the monuments in this area are to the 'virtuous dead' of both Britain and [[ancient Greece]].{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=17}}

The buildings in this area are:

==== St Mary's Church ====

In the woods between the House and the Elysian Fields is Stowe [[parish church]]. This is the only surviving structure from the old village of Stowe. Dating from the 14th century, the building consists of a [[nave]] with [[aisle]]s and a west tower, a [[chancel]] with a chapel to the north and an east window c. 1300 with [[wikt:reticulate|reticulated]] [[tracery]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Stuff |first=Good |title=Parish Church of St Mary, Stowe, Buckinghamshire |url=https://britishlistedbuildings.co.uk/101211870-parish-church-of-st-mary-stowe |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=britishlistedbuildings.co.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144613/https://britishlistedbuildings.co.uk/101211870-parish-church-of-st-mary-stowe |url-status=live }}</ref>

Lancelot "Capability" Brown was married in the church in 1744.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=89}} The church contains a fine [[Laurence Whistler]] etched glass window in memory of The Hon. Mrs. Thomas Close-Smith of Boycott Manor, eldest daughter of the [[11th Lady Kinloss]], who was the eldest daughter of the [[3rd Duke of Buckingham and Chandos]]. Thomas Close-Smith himself was the [[High Sheriff of Buckinghamshire]] in 1942, and died in 1946.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stowe Parish Church {{!}} HISTORY |url=https://stowechurch.org.uk/church-history/ |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=stowechurch.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144614/https://stowechurch.org.uk/church-history/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Caroline Mary, his wife, known as May, died in 1972.<ref>{{cite web |title=Stowe Church web site |url=http://stowechurch.org.uk/history.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060107104949/http://stowechurch.org.uk/history.html |archive-date=2006-01-07 |website=stowechurch.org.uk}}</ref>

==== The Temple of Ancient Virtue ====

Built in 1737 to the designs of Kent, in the form of a [[Tholos (Ancient Rome)|Tholos]], a circular domed building surrounded by columns.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=89}} In this case they are unfluted Ionic columns, 16 in number, raised on a podium. There are twelve steps up to the two arched doorless entrances. Above the entrances are the words ''Priscae virtuti'' (to Ancient Virtue).{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=26}}  Within are four niches one between the two doorways. They contain four life size sculptures (plaster copies of the originals by [[Peter Scheemakers]] paid for in 1737, they were sold in 1921). They are [[Epaminondas]] (general), [[Lycurgus of Sparta|Lycurgus]] (lawmaker), [[Homer]] (poet) and [[Socrates]] (philosopher).{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=90}}

==== The Temple of British Worthies ====

Designed by Kent and built 1734–1735. Built of stone, it is a curving roofless [[exedra]] with a large stone pier in the centre surmounted by a stepped pyramid containing an oval niche that contains a bust of [[Mercury (mythology)|Mercury]], a copy of the original. The curving wall contains six [[Niche (architecture)|niches]] either side of the central pier, with further niches on the two ends of the wall and two more behind.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=28-30}} At the back of the Temple is a chamber with an arched entrance, dedicated to Signor Fido, a greyhound.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=93-4}}{{refn|His death is marked with an epitaph, the first and last lines of which read:{{poemquote|To the memory of Signor Fido an Italian of good Extraction, who came to England not to bite us, like most of his countrymen, but to gain an honest livelihood. He hunted not after fame, yet acqui'd it: ...
Reader, this stone is guiltless of Flattery; for he to whom it is inscrib'd was not a Man but a [[Greyhound]].}}|name=second|group=lower-alpha}}

The niches are filled by [[Bust (sculpture)|busts]], half of which were carved by [[John Michael Rysbrack]] for a previous building in the gardens. They portray [[John Milton]], [[William Shakespeare]], [[John Locke]], Sir [[Isaac Newton]], Sir [[Francis Bacon]], [[Elizabeth I]], [[William III of England|William III]] and [[Inigo Jones]]. The other eight are by [[Peter Scheemakers]], which were commissioned especially for the Temple. These represent [[Alexander Pope]], Sir [[Thomas Gresham]], King [[Alfred the Great]], [[Edward the Black Prince]], Sir [[Walter Raleigh]], Sir [[Francis Drake]], [[John Hampden]] and Sir [[John Barnard (British politician)|John Barnard]] ([[Whigs (British political party)|Whig]] [[Member of Parliament|MP]] and opponent of the Whig Prime Minister Sir [[Robert Walpole]]).{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=92}}

[[File:Temple of British Worthies Elizabeth I.jpg|thumb|183x183px|Temple of British Worthies, Elizabeth I]]

The choice of who was considered a 'British Worthy' was very much influenced by the Whig politics of the family, the chosen individuals falling into two groups, eight known for their actions and eight known for their thoughts and ideas.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=91}} The only woman to be included was Elizabeth I.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Patriots in the landscape: Art UK |url=https://artuk.org/discover/curations/patriots-in-the-landscape/template/storyline |access-date=2023-01-26 |website=artuk.org |archive-date=23 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230123110028/https://artuk.org/discover/curations/patriots-in-the-landscape/template/storyline |url-status=live }}</ref> The inscription above her bust, which praises her leadership, reads:
{{Blockquote|text=Who confounded the Projects, and destroyed the Power, that threatened to oppress the Liberties of Europe... and, by a wise, a moderate, and a popular Government, gave Wealth, Security, and Respect to England}}

==== The Shell Bridge ====

Designed by Kent, and finished by 1739, is actually a dam disguised as a bridge of five arches and is decorated with shells.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=31}}{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=94}}

==== The Grotto ====

Probably designed by Kent in the 1730s, is located at the head of the serpentine 'river Styx' that flows through the Elysian Fields. There are two pavilions, one ornamented with shells the other with pebbles and flints. In the central room is a circular recess in which are two basins of white marble.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=31-32}} In the upper is a marble statue of Venus rising from her bath, and water falls from the upper into the lower basin, there passing under the floor to the front, where it falls into the river Styx. A tablet of marble is inscribed with these lines from [[John Milton]]:{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=96}}

{{poemquote|Goddess of the silver wave,
To thy thick embower'd cave,
To arched walks, and twilight groves,
And shadows brown, which Sylvan loves
When the sun begins to fling
His flaring beams, me, Goddess, bring.}}

==== The Seasons Fountain ====

Probably erected in 1805, built from white statuary marble. Spring water flows from it, and the basic structure appears to be made from an 18th-century chimneypiece.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=95}} It used to be decorated with [[Wedgwood]] plaques of the four seasons and had silver drinking cups suspended on either side.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=32}} it was the first structure to be reconstructed under National Trust ownership.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=95}}

==== The Grenville Column ====

Originally erected in 1749 near the Grecian Valley, it was moved to its present location in 1756; Earl Temple probably designed it.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=32-33}} It commemorates one of Lord Cobham's nephews, Captain [[Thomas Grenville (sailor)|Thomas Grenville]] [[Royal Navy|RN]].{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=88-9}} He was killed in 1747 while fighting the French off [[Cape Finisterre]] aboard HMS ''[[HMS Defiance (1744)|Defiance]]'' under the command of [[George Anson, 1st Baron Anson|Admiral Anson]].{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=32-33}}

The monument is based on an [[Ancient Roman]] naval monument, a [[rostral column]], one that is carved with the prows of Roman galleys sticking out from the shaft. The order used is Tuscan, and is surmounted by a statue of [[Calliope]] holding a scroll inscribed ''Non nisi grandia canto'' (Only sing of heroic deeds); there is a lengthy inscription in Latin added to the base of the column after it was moved.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=32-33}}

==== The Cook Monument ====

Built in 1778 as a monument to Captain [[James Cook]]; it takes the form of a stone globe on a pedestal. It was moved to its present position in 1842.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=95}} The pedestal has a carved relief of Cook's head in profile and the inscription ''Jacobo Cook/MDCCLXXVIII''.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=33}}

==== The Gothic Cross ====

Erected in 1814 from [[Coade stone]] on the path linking the Doric Arch to the Temple of Ancient Virtue. It was erected by the 1st Duke of Buckingham and Chandos as a memorial to his mother Lady Mary Nugent.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=88-9}} It was demolished in the 1980s by a falling [[elm]] tree. The National Trust rebuilt the cross in 2016 using several of the surviving pieces of the monument.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MNA130267: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA130267 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144630/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA130267 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== The Marquess of Buckingham's Urn ====

Sited behind the Temple of British Worthies, erected in 1814 by the 1st Duke in memory of his father, the urn was moved to the school precincts in 1931. A replica urn was created and erected in 2018.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MNA129622: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129622 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144616/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129622 |url-status=live }}</ref>

