=== Hawkwell Field ===

[[File:Stowe_crop2.JPG|right|thumb|Stowe Gardens, The Hawkwell Field, with the [[Palladian]] Bridge on the right and the [[Gothic architecture|Gothic]] Temple on the rising ground straight ahead. Beyond, the [[Queen Charlotte|Queen's]] Temple and [[Richard Temple, 1st Viscount Cobham|Lord Cobham's]] Monument are just visible.]]

Hawkwell Field lies to the east of the Elysian Fields, and is also known as ''The Eastern Garden''. This area of the gardens was developed in the 1730s & 1740s, an open area surrounded by some of the larger buildings all designed by [[James Gibbs]].{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=80}}

The buildings in this area are:

==== The Queen's Temple ====

Originally designed by Gibbs in 1742 and was then called the ''Lady's Temple''.<ref name=":7">{{Cite web |title=MNA129609: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129609 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk  |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144619/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129609 |url-status=live }}</ref> This was designed for Lady Cobham to entertain her friends. But the building was extensively remodelled in 1772–1774 to give it a neo-classical form.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=42-43}}

Further alterations were made in 1790 by Vincenzo Valdrè.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=101-02}} These commemorated the recovery of [[George III]] from madness with the help of Queen [[Charlotte of Mecklenburg-Strelitz|Charlotte]] after whom the building was renamed.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=143}}

The main floor is raised up on a podium, the main façade consists of a portico of four fluted [[Composite order|Composite]] columns, these are approached by a balustraded flight of steps the width of the portico. The façade is wider than the portico, the flanking walls having niches containing ornamental urns. The large door is fully glazed.<ref name=":7" />

The room within is the most elaborately decorated of any of the garden's buildings. The [[Scagliola]] Corinthian columns and pilasters are based on the [[Temple of Venus and Roma]], the barrel-vaulted ceiling is coffered. There are several plaster medallions around the walls, including: [[Britannia]] Deject, with this inscription ''Desideriis icta fidelibus Quaerit Patria Caesarem'' (For Caesar's life, with anxious hopes and fears Britannia lifts to Heaven a nation's tears); Britannia with a palm branch sacrificing to [[Aesculapius]] with this inscription ''O Sol pulcher! O laudande, Canam recepto Caesare felix'' (Oh happy days! with rapture Britons sing the day when Heavenrestore their favourite King!); Britannia supporting a medallion of the Queen with the inscription ''Charlottae Sophiae Augustae, Pietate erga Regem, erga Rempublicam Virtute et constantia, In difficillimis temporibus spectatissimae D.D.D. Georgius M. de Buckingham MDCCLXXXIX''. (To the Queen, Most respectable in the most difficult moments, for her attachment and zeal for the public service, George Marquess of Buckingham dedicates this monument).<ref name=":7" />

Other plaster decoration on the walls includes: 1. Trophies of Religion, Justice and Mercy, 2. Agriculture and Manufacture, 3. Navigation and Commerce and 4. War.<ref name=":7" /> Almost all the decoration was the work of Charles Peart except for the statue of [[Britannia]] by [[Giuseppe Ceracchi]].

In 1842 the 2nd Duke of Buckingham inserted in the centre of the floor the Roman [[mosaic]] found at nearby [[Foscott, Buckinghamshire|Foscott]].<ref name=":7" /> The Temple has been used for over 40 years by the school as its Music School.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=101-02}}

==== The Gothic Temple ====

Designed by [[James Gibbs]] in 1741 and completed about 1748, the Gothic Temple is the gardens' only building of [[ironstone]]; all the others use a creamy-yellow [[limestone]]. The two-storey building is triangular in plan, with a pentagonal tower at each corner: one rises two floors higher than the main building, while the others have lanterns on their roofs. Above the door is a quote from [[Pierre Corneille]]'s play ''Horace'': "''Je rends grace aux Dieux de n'estre pas Roman''" (I thank the gods I am not a Roman).{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=36-37}}

The interior includes a circular room of two storeys covered by a shallow dome that is painted to mimic mosaic work including shields representing the [[Heptarchy]]. Dedicated 'To the Liberty of our Ancestors'. To quote [[John Martin Robinson]]: 'to the Whigs, [[Anglo-Saxons|Saxon]] and Gothic were interchangeably associated with freedom and ancient English liberties: [[Jury trial|trial by jury]] (erroneously thought to have been founded by King Alfred at a moot on Salisbury Plain), [[Magna Carta]], parliamentary representation, all the things which the [[English Civil War|Civil War]] and [[Glorious Revolution]] had protected from the wiles of [[House of Stuart|Stuart]] would-be [[Absolute monarchy|absolutism]], and to the preservation of which Lord Cobham and his 'Patriots' were seriously devoted.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=102}}

The Temple was used in the 1930s by the school as the [[Officer Training Corps]] armoury.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=104}} It is now available as a holiday let through the [[Landmark Trust]].<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Matthews |first1=Helen |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=u_OJDwAAQBAJ&dq=gothic+temple+landmark+trust&pg=PA243 |title=Slow Travel: The Chilterns & the Thames Valley |last2=Matthews |first2=Neil |date=2019-02-15 |publisher=Bradt Travel Guides |isbn=978-1-78477-613-8 |page=243 |access-date=2022-07-27 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044917/https://books.google.com/books?id=u_OJDwAAQBAJ&dq=gothic+temple+landmark+trust&pg=PA243 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== The Temple of Friendship ====

Built of stone in 1739 to the designs of Gibbs.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=38-40}} It is located in the south-east corner of the garden. Inscribed on the exterior of the building is AMICITIAE S (sacred to friendship). It was badly damaged by fire in 1840 and remains a ruin.<ref name=":8">{{Cite web |title=MNA129275: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129275 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144623/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129275 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Built as a pavilion to entertain Lord Cobham's friends it was originally decorated with [[mural]]s by Francesco Sleter including on the ceiling [[Britannia]], the walls having [[Allegory|allegorical]] paintings symbolising friendship, justice and liberty. There was a series of ten white marble busts on black marble pedestals around the walls of Cobham (this bust with that of Lord Westmoreland is now in the V&A Museum) and his friends: [[Frederick, Prince of Wales]]; [[Philip Stanhope, 4th Earl of Chesterfield]]; [[George Lyttelton, 1st Baron Lyttelton]]; [[Thomas Fane, 8th Earl of Westmorland]]; William Pitt, 1st Earl of Chatham; [[Allen Bathurst, 1st Earl Bathurst]]; Richard Grenville-Temple, 2nd Earl Temple; [[Alexander Hume-Campbell, 2nd Earl of Marchmont]]; [[John Leveson-Gower, 1st Earl Gower]]. Dated 1741, three were carved by Peter Scheemakers: Cobham, Prince Frederick & Lord Chesterfield, the rest were carved by [[List of monumental masons|Thomas Adye]]. All the busts were sold in 1848.<ref name=":8"/>

The building consisted of a square room rising through two floors surmounted by a pyramidal roof with a lantern. The front has a [[portico]] of four [[Tuscan order|Tuscan]] columns supporting a pediment, the sides have [[Arcade (architecture)|arcades]] of one arch deep by three wide also supporting pediments. The arcades and portico with the wall behind are still standing.<ref name=":8"/>

==== The Palladian Bridge ====

This is a copy of the bridge at [[Wilton House]] in [[Wiltshire]], which was itself based in a design by [[Andrea Palladio]]. The main difference from the Wilton version, which is a footbridge, is that the Stowe version is designed to be used by horse-drawn carriages so is set lower with shallow ramps instead of steps on the approach. It was completed in 1738 probably under the direction of Gibbs. Of five arches, the central wide and segmental with carved [[Keystone (architecture)|keystone]], the two flanking semi-circular also with carved keystones, the two outer segmental. There is a [[balustrade]]d [[parapet]], the middle three arches also supporting an open pavilion. Above the central arch this consists of [[colonnade]]s of four full and two half columns of unfluted Roman Ionic order. Above the flanking arches there are pavilions with arches on all four sides. These have engaged columns on their flanks and ends of the same order as the colonnade which in turn support pediments. The roof is of slate, with an elaborate plaster ceiling. It originally crossed a stream that emptied from the ''Octagon Lake'', and when the lake was enlarged and deepened, made more natural in shape in 1752, this part of the stream became a branch of the lake.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=40-42}}

==== The Saxon Deities ====

These are sculptures by [[John Michael Rysbrack]] of the seven deities that gave their names to the days of the week.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=102}} Carved from [[Portland stone]] in 1727. They were moved to their present location in 1773. (The sculptures are copies of the originals that were sold in 1921–1922).<ref name=":9">{{Cite web |title=MNA129271: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129271 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144621/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129271 |url-status=live }}</ref> For those, like the Grenville family, who followed [[Whigs (British political party)|Whig politics]], the terms 'Saxon' and 'Gothic' represented supposedly English liberties, such as trial by jury.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=102}}

The sculptures are arranged in a circle. Each sculpture (with the exception of Sunna a half length sculpture) is life size, the base of each statue has a [[Runic alphabet|Runic]] inscription of the god's name, and stands on a plinth. They are: [[Sól (sun)|Sunna]] (Sunday), [[Máni|Mona]] (Monday), [[Týr|Tiw]] (Tuesday), [[Woden]] (Wednesday), [[Thor|Thuner]] (Thursday), [[Frigg|Friga]] (Friday) and a Saxon version of [[Saturn (mythology)|Seatern]] (Saturday).<ref name=":9" />

The original Sunna & Thuner statues are in the V&A Museum, the original Friga stood for many years in [[Portmeirion]] but was sold at auction in 1994 for £54,000, the original Mona is in the [[Buckinghamshire County Museum]].{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=43-44}}

