=== The Grecian Valley ===

[[File:The Temple of Concord and Victory, Stowe - geograph.org.uk - 836606.jpg|right|thumb|The Grecian Valley looking towards the Temple of Concord and Victory]]

This area is to the north of the Eastern Garden. Designed by Capability Brown and created from 1747 to 1749, this is Brown's first known landscape design. An L-shaped area of lawns covering about {{cvt|60|acre}}, was formed by excavating {{cvt|23500|cuyd}} of earth by hand and removed in [[wheelbarrow]]s with the original intention of creating a lake. Mature [[Tilia|Lime]] and Elm trees were [[Digging trees and shrubs for transplanting|transplanted]] from elsewhere on the estate to create a mature landscape. Other tree species that Brown used in this and other areas of the gardens include: [[Cedar wood|cedar]], [[Taxus baccata|yew]], beech, [[Acer pseudoplatanus|sycamore]], [[larch]] & [[Scots Pine|Scots pine]].{{sfn|Robinson|1990|pp=106-107}} As of 2020 there was large [[Platanus × hispanica|London plane]] tree in the Grecian Valley, that was potentially planted by Capability Brown.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Woolf |first=Jo |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-0UmEAAAQBAJ&dq=%22planting+scheme%22+stowe&pg=PT194 |title=Britain's Trees: A Treasury of Traditions, Superstitions, Remedies and Literature |date=2020-04-01 |publisher=National Trust |isbn=978-1-911657-17-0 |access-date=23 October 2022 |archive-date=9 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230309015355/https://books.google.com/books?id=-0UmEAAAQBAJ&dq=%22planting+scheme%22+stowe&pg=PT194 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The buildings in this area are:

==== Temple of Concord and Victory ====

The designer of this, the largest of the garden buildings, is unknown, although both Earl Temple and Thomas Pitt, 1st Baron Camelford have been suggested as the architect. It is a highly significant, since it is the first building in England whose design intentionally imitate Greek architecture; it was originally known as the 'Grecian Temple'.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|pp=97-8}} Earl Temple was a member of the [[Society of Dilettanti]], a group made up of members of the aristocracy who pursued the study of art and architecture.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=107}}

Built from stone, between 1747 and 1749, the building is located where the two legs of the valley meet. It is raised on a [[podium]] with a flight of steps up to the main entrance, the [[cella]] and [[pronaos]] is surrounded by a [[peristyle]] of 28 fluted [[Ionic order|Roman Ionic]] columns, ten on the flanks and six at each end.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=47-50}} The main [[pediment]] contains a sculpture by [[Peter Scheemakers]] of ''Four-Quarters of the World bringing their Various Products to Britannia.''{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=97-8}} There are six statues [[acroterion]] of cast lead painted to resemble stone on both the east and west pediments. In the [[frieze]] of the [[entablature]] are the words ''CONCORDIAE ET VICTORIAE.''{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=47-50}}

The sculpture on the building dates from the 1760s when it was converted into a monument to the British victory in the [[Seven Years' War]].{{sfn|Bevington|1994|pp=97-8}} The ceiling of the peristyle is based on an engraving by [[Robert Wood (engraver)|Robert Wood]] of a ceiling in [[Palmyra]]. Within the pronaos and cella are 16 [[terracotta]] medallions commemorating British Victories in the Seven Years' War, these were designed by [[James "Athenian" Stuart]], each one is inscribed with the name of the battle: [[Battle of the Plains of Abraham|Quebec]]; [[Invasion of Martinique (1762)|Martinico & c.]]; [[Siege of Louisbourg (1758)|Louisbourg]]; [[Invasion of Guadeloupe (1759)|Guadeloupe & c.]]; [[Montreal campaign|Montreal]]; [[Puducherry (city)|Pondicherry & c.]]; the naval battle of [[Belleisle, Nova Scotia|Belleisle]]; the naval [[Battle of Lagos]]; [[Battle of Minden|Crevelt & Minden]]; [[Battle of Villinghausen|Fellinghausen]]; [[Capture of Gorée|Goree]] and [[Capture of Senegal|Senegal]]; [[Fort Crown Point|Crown Point]], [[Battle of Fort Niagara|Niagara]] and [[Fort Duquesne|Quesne]]; [[Siege of Havana|Havannah]] and [[British occupation of Manila|Manila]]; [[Battle of Fort Beauséjour|Beau Sejour]], [[Raid on Cherbourg|Cherburgh]] and [[Capture of Belle Île|Belleisle]].{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=47-50}}

The wooden doors are painted a [[Prussian blue]] with gilded highlights on the mouldings. Above the door is an inscription by [[Valerius Maximus]]:

{{verse translation|Quo Tempore Salus eorum in ultimas Ausustias deducta
nullum Ambitioni Locum relinquebat|The Times with such alarming Dangers fraught
Left not a Hope for any factious Thought|lang=la}}

The interior end wall of the cella has an [[aedicule]] containing a statue of [[Liberty (goddess)|Liberty]].<ref name=":10">{{Cite web |title=MNA129886: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129886 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144632/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129886 |url-status=live }}</ref> Above is this inscription:

{{verse translation|Candidis autem animis voluptatem praebuerint in
cinspicuo posita qua cuique magnifica merito contigerunt|A sweet sensation touches every breast of candour's generous sentiment possest,
When public services with honour due, are gratefully marked out to public view|lang=la}}

The six statues from the roof were sold in 1921.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=98}} When the school built its chapel in the late 1920s, 16 of the 28 columns from this Temple were moved to the new building, being replaced with plain brickwork. From 1994 to 1996 the National Trust undertook restoration works to create replacement columns with which to restore the Temple.<ref name=":10" />

==== The Fane of Pastoral Poetry ====

Located in a grove of trees at the eastern end of the Grecian Valley, at the north-east corner of the gardens, the structure is a small [[Belvedere (structure)|belvedere]] designed by James Gibbs in 1729.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=50}} It was moved to its present position in the 1760s; it originally stood where Queen Caroline's statue stands.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=99}} It is square in plan with chamfered corners that, built of stone, each side is an open arch, [[herma]] protrude from each chamfered corner. It is surmounted by an octagonal lead dome.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=50}}

==== The Circle of the Dancing Faun ====

Located near the north-east end of the valley near the Fane of Pastoral Poetry, the Dancing Faun commanded the centre of a circle of five sculptures of shepherds and shepherdesses, all of the sculptures had been sold. Two of these statues were located in Buckingham and restored in 2009 to their original place in the garden. In 2016, the [[Faun]] supported by the so-called Saxon Altar and the other three statues were recreated.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MNA129798: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129798 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144618/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129798 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== The Cobham Monument ====

To the south of the Grecian Valley is the tallest structure in the gardens rising 115&nbsp;ft.<ref name=":11">{{Cite web |title=MNA129639: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129639 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144624/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129639 |url-status=live }}</ref> Built 1747–49 of stone, probably designed by Brown, who adapted a design by Gibbs.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|pp=100-101}} It consists of a square plinth with corner buttresses surmounted by [[Coade stone]] lions holding shields added in 1778. The column itself is octagonal with a single [[Fluting (architecture)|flute]] on each face, with a molded [[Doric order|doric]] capital and base. On which is a small belvedere of eight arches with a dome supporting the sculpture of Lord Cobham, the probable sculptor of which was Peter Scheemakers.<ref name=":11" />

The present statue is a recreation made in 2001 after the original was destroyed by [[lightning]] in 1957.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|pp=100-101}} A spiral staircase rises through the column to the belvedere, providing an elevated view of the gardens. Lord Cobham's Walk is a tree-lined avenue that stretches from the Pillar north-east to the edge of the gardens.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=50}}

==== Statues Of The Grecian Valley ====

The National Trust is creating copies of the statues that were once found around the edge of the Grecian Valley, and is adding them when funds can be raised to cover the cost of recreating them.<ref name=":5"/> The sculptures included [[Samson]] and the Philistine recreated in 2015, and several of the twelve [[Labours of Hercules]] – so far only Hercules and Antaeus has been recreated (in 2016), and a statue of a [[gladiator]] in 2017.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MNA129204: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129204 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144627/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129204 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=MNA129876: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129876 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144617/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129876 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=MNA130499: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA130499 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144629/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA130499 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 2018, a replacement of the statue of [[Thalia (Muse)|Thalia]] holding a scroll with the words ''Pastorum Carmina Canto'' on it was erected near the Fane of Pastoral Poetry; the statue is based on a work by [[John Nost]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=MNA131362: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA131362 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144628/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA131362 |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2019, a copy of the Grecian Urn sold in 1921 and now at [[Trent Park]] in north London has been erected near the Circle of the Dancing Faun.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MNA129382: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129382 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144622/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129382 |url-status=live }}</ref>

