=== Western Gardens ===

To the immediate west of the South Vista are the Western Gardens, which include the ''Eleven-Acre Lake''.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=75}} This area of the gardens was developed from 1712 to 1770s when it underwent its final landscaping. The ''Eleven-acre lake'' was extended and given a natural shape in 1762.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=113}} In the woods to the north-west in 2017 the National Trust recreated the lost sculpture of the [[Wrestlers (sculpture)|Wrestlers]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Wrestlers return to Stowe |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/the-wrestlers-return-to-stowe |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=National Trust |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144619/https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/the-wrestlers-return-to-stowe |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2018 the paths surrounding the sculpture were recreated and the Labyrinth around them replanted with 3,500 shrubs including [[magnolia]], [[Laurus nobilis|laurel]], [[Buxus|box]], [[yew]], [[Euonymus japonicus|spindle]] and [[hazel]]. Within the labyrinth are an outdoor [[Skittles (sport)|skittle alley]] and a rustic [[Swing (seat)|swing]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Revealing the Labyrinth at Stowe |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/revealing-the-labyrinth-at-stowe |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=National Trust |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144614/https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/revealing-the-labyrinth-at-stowe |url-status=live }}</ref>

Also in this area in the woods to the north of the lake but on the east side is the ''Sleeping Wood'' designed by Bridgeman. At the heart of it once stood the ''Sleeping Parlour'', built in 1725 to a design by Vanbrugh. This was inspired by [[Charles Perrault]]'s tale of [[Sleeping Beauty]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Sleeping Wood at Stowe |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/the-sleeping-wood-at-stowe |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=National Trust |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144615/https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/the-sleeping-wood-at-stowe |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[File:Pegg's Walk, Stowe Landscape Gardens - geograph.org.uk - 837193.jpg|thumb|Pegg's Terrace]]

Pegg's Terrace is a raised avenue of trees that follows the line of the south ha-ha between the Lake Pavilions and the Temple of Venus. Warden Hill Walk, also a raised avenue of trees, is on the western edge of the gardens, the southern part of which serves as a dam for the Eleven Acre Lake, links The Temple of Venus to the Boycott Pavilions.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stowe missing monuments walk |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/trails/stowe-missing-monuments-walk |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=National Trust |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144617/https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/trails/stowe-missing-monuments-walk |url-status=live }}</ref>

The buildings in this area are:

==== The Rotondo ====

Designed by Vanbrugh and built 1720–1721, this is a circular temple, consisting of ten unfluted Roman Ionic columns raised up on a podium of three steps. The dome was altered by Borra in 1773–1774 to give it a lower profile. In the centre is a statue of [[Venus (mythology)|Venus]] raised on a tall decorated plinth, which is replacement for the original and is [[Gilding|gilt]].{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=13-15}} The building was modelled on the temple of Venus at [[Knidos]].{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=85}}

==== Statue of Queen Caroline ====

This takes the form of a [[Tetrapylon]], a high square plinth surmounted by four fluted Roman Ionic columns supporting an [[entablature]] which in turn supports the statue of Queen [[Caroline of Brandenburg-Ansbach|Caroline]]. On its pedestal is inscribed ''Honori, Laudi, Virtuti Divae Carolinae'' (To honour, Praise and Virtue of the Divine Caroline). According to the authors of National Trust's 1997 guidebook, it was probably designed by Vanbrugh.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=15-16}} It stands on the mound left by a former ice house.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=84}}

==== The Temple of Venus ====

Dated 1731 this was the first building in the gardens designed by [[William Kent]]. Located in the south-west corner of the gardens on the far side of the Eleven-Acre Lake. The stone building takes the form of one of [[Palladio]]'s villas, the central rectangular room linked by two quadrant arcades to pavilions.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=16-17}} According to Michael Bevington, it was an early example of architecture being inspired by that of [[Thermae|Roman baths]].{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=82}}

The main [[pediment]]ed façade has an [[exedra]] screened by two full and two half Roman Ionic columns, there are two niches containing busts either side of the door of [[Cleopatra]] & [[Faustina the Elder|Faustina]], the exedra is flanked by two niches containing busts of [[Nero]] and [[Vespasian]] all people known for their sexual appetites. The end pavilions have domes. Above the door is carved VENERI HORTENSI "to Venus of the garden".{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=82}}

The interior according to the 1756 Seeley Guidebook was decorated with murals painted by [[Francesco Sleter]], the centre of the ceiling had a painting of a naked [[Venus (mythology)|Venus]] and the smaller compartments were painted with a "variety of intrigues". The walls had paintings with scenes from [[Edmund Spenser]]'s ''[[The Faerie Queene]]''.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=82}} The paintings were destroyed in the late 18th century.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MNA129136: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129136 |access-date=2022-08-25 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220825144614/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA129136 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The ceiling frieze had this inscription from the [[Pervigilium Veneris]]:{{verse translation|Nunc amet qui nondum amavit:
Quique amavit, nunc amet.|Let him love now, who never lov'd before:
Let him, who always lov'd, now love the more.|lang=la}}

==== The Hermitage ====

Designed c.1731 by Kent, heavily [[Rustication (architecture)|rusticated]] and with a pediment containing a carving of panpipes within a wreath, and a small tower to the right of the entrance.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=19-20}} It never housed a [[hermit]].{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=82}}

==== Dido's Cave ====

This small alcove, built in the 1720s, was originally decorated with a painting of [[Dido (Queen of Carthage)|Dido]] and [[Aeneas]].{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=19}} In c.1781 the dressed stone façade was replaced with [[tufa]] by the Marchioness of Buckingham.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|pp=86-7}} Her son the 1st Duke of Buckingham turned it into her memorial by adding the inscription ''Mater Amata, Vale!'' (Farewell beloved Mother).{{sfn|Bevington|1994|pp=86-7}} The designer is unknown.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=19}}

==== The Boycott Pavilions ====

Built of stone and designed by [[James Gibbs]], the eastern one was built in 1728 and the western in 1729. They are named after the nearby vanished hamlet of [[Boycott, Buckinghamshire|Boycott]]. Located on the brow of a hill overlooking the river Dad, they flank the Oxford drive. Originally both were in the form of square planned open [[Belvedere (structure)|belvederes]] with stone pyramidal roofs. In 1758 the architect [[Giovanni Battista Borra]] altered them, replacing them with the lead domes, with a round [[dormer window]] in each face and an open [[roof lantern]] in the centre. The eastern pavilion was converted into a three-storey house in 1952.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=10-11}}

