=== The Lamport Gardens ===

[[File:Lamport Garden (Stowe) - Buckinghamshire, England - DSC06720.jpg|thumb|Waterfall Lamport Gardens]]

Lying to the east of the Eastern Gardens, this was the last area of the gardens to be created and is the smallest at just 17 acres (6.9 ha). Named after the vanished hamlet of [[Lamport, Buckinghamshire|Lamport]], the gardens were created in 1826 by [[Richard Temple-Grenville, 1st Duke of Buckingham and Chandos]] and his gardener James Brown. From 1840, [[Richard Temple-Grenville, 2nd Duke of Buckingham and Chandos|2nd Duke of Buckingham]]'s gardener, Mr Ferguson, and the architect [[Edward Blore]], adapted it as an ornamental [[Garden design#Rock garden|rock]] and [[water garden]]. Originally the garden was stocked with exotic birds including [[emu]]s.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=105}}

The buildings in this area are:

=== The Chinese House ===

The Chinese House is known to date from 1738, making it the first known building in England built in the [[Chinese architecture|Chinese style]].{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=45}} It is made of wood and painted on canvas inside and out by [[Francesco Sleter]]. Originally, it was built on stilts in a pond near the Elysian Fields. In 1751, it was moved from Stowe and reconstructed first at [[Wotton House]], the nearby seat of the Grenville family. In 1951, it was then moved to [[Harristown House, County Kildare]] in Ireland.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=91}} Its construction set a new fashion in landscape gardening for Chinese-inspired structures.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Avcioglu |first=Nebahat |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ynW1TFPfe1kC&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PA148 |title='Turquerie' and the Politics of Representation, 1728-1876 |date=2011 |publisher=Ashgate Publishing |isbn=978-0-7546-6422-2 |page=147 |access-date=23 October 2022 |archive-date=9 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230309015343/https://books.google.com/books?id=ynW1TFPfe1kC&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PA148 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Braham |first=Allan |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=EstKYtJpOK0C&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PA70 |title=The Architecture of the French Enlightenment |date=1989-01-01 |publisher=University of California Press |isbn=978-0-520-06739-4 |page=71 |access-date=23 October 2022 |archive-date=9 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230309015409/https://books.google.com/books?id=EstKYtJpOK0C&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PA70 |url-status=live }}</ref>

It was purchased by the National Trust in 1996 and returned to its original current position at Stowe. The Chinoiserie Garden Pavilion at [[Hamilton Gardens]] in New Zealand is based on the Chinese House at Stowe.<ref name="chinoiseriegarden">{{cite web |title=Chinoiserie Garden |url=http://hamiltongardens.co.nz/collections/fantasy-collection/chinoiserie-garden/ |access-date=26 September 2015 |website=hamiltongardens.co.nz |publisher=Hamilton Gardens |archive-date=27 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150927064350/http://hamiltongardens.co.nz/collections/fantasy-collection/chinoiserie-garden/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

