==== Estate Buildings ====

Buildings on the wider estate, both on current and former land-holdings, include:
* Stowe Castle (Not owned by the National Trust) is two miles (3&nbsp;km) to the east of the gardens, built in the 1730s probably to designs by Gibbs. The tall [[Curtain wall (fortification)|curtain wall]] visible from the gardens actually disguises several farmworkers' cottages.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=131}}
[[File:Stowe Bourbon Tower.jpg|thumb|upright|The Bourbon Tower prior to restoration]]
[[File:Stowe, Wolfe's Obelisk - geograph.org.uk - 152791.jpg|thumb|upright|The Wolfe Obelisk]]
* The Bourbon Tower, approximately one thousand feet to the east of the Lamport Garden, was built c1741 probably to designs by Gibbs, it is a circular tower of three floors with a conical roof, it was given its present name in 1808 to commemorate a visit by the exiled [[House of Bourbon|French royal family]].{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=130}}
* The 2nd Duke's Obelisk near the Bourbon Tower, this [[granite]] [[obelisk]] was erected in 1864.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=130}}
* The Wolfe Obelisk stone {{cvt|100|ft|m}} high located about {{cvt|2000|ft|m}} to the north-west of the garden, originally designed by Vanbrugh, it was moved in 1754 from the centre of the Octagon Lake and is a memorial to [[General Wolfe]].{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=130}}
* The Gothic Umbrello, also called the Conduit House. It houses beneath its floor a [[Waterway|conduit]]. About a {{cvt|1000|ft|m}} south of the Wolfe Obelisk, is a small octagonal pavilion dating from the 1790s. The coat of arms of the Marquess of Buckingham, dated 1793, made from [[Coade stone]] are placed over the entrance door.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=129}}
* Silverstone Lodges (Not owned by the National Trust), built by the 1st Duke, these twin lodges once flanked the northern entrance to the park, and used to lead to the private carriage drive from [[Silverstone]] to the house. The drive no longer exists and has long since been destroyed. Part of it passed through what is now the racing circuit.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=131}}

=== North Front ===

[[File:Statue of George I, Stowe - geograph.org.uk - 837967.jpg|thumb|upright|The Statue of George I]]The North Front of Stowe School is closed to visitors. In front of the north façade of the house, the forecourt has in its centre an:

====Equestrian Statue of George I====

This is a greater than life size [[equestrian statue]] of [[George I of Great Britain|George I]] by [[Andries Carpentière]], located in the middle of the Forecourt, made of cast [[lead]] in 1723. It is on a tall stone plinth. It was this monarch that gave Lord Cobham his title of viscount in 1718 and restored his military command, leading to his involvement in the [[Capture of Vigo]].{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=12}}

==== The Menagerie ====

Hidden in the woods to the west of the South Vista. It was built by the Marquess of Buckingham for his wife as a retreat.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|pp=87-8}} It was built in stone, c.1781, probably to the designs of Valdrè.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=21}} The 1st Duke converted it to a museum where he displayed his collections, which included a {{cvt|32|ft|m}} long [[Boa constrictor]] - at the time the largest in England.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|pp=87-8}} The building is in private use by Stowe School.<ref>{{Cite web |title=0420802006 - DOMED PAVILION, School Shop, Stowe School - Buckinghamshire's Heritage Portal |url=https://heritageportal.buckinghamshire.gov.uk/Monument/MBC11228 |access-date=2022-08-30 |website=heritageportal.buckinghamshire.gov.uk |archive-date=2022-08-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220830081750/https://heritageportal.buckinghamshire.gov.uk/Monument/MBC11228 |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Lost buildings and monuments ==

As the design of the gardens evolved, many changes were made. This resulted in the demolition of many monuments. The following is a list by area of such monuments.

;The Approaches
* The Chackmore Fountain. Built c.1831, was situated halfway down the Grand Avenue near the [[Hamlet (place)|hamlet]] of [[Chackmore]]. It was dismantled in the 1950s.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MNA130010: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA130010 |access-date=2022-08-30 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220830084945/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA130010 |url-status=live }}</ref> It was photographed by [[John Piper (artist)|John Piper]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Tate |title='Photograph of Chackmore Fountain in Stowe, Buckinghamshire', John Piper, [c.1930s–1980s] – Tate Archive |url=https://www.tate.org.uk/art/archive/items/tga-8728-1-3-102/piper-photograph-of-chackmore-fountain-in-stowe-buckinghamshire |access-date=2022-08-30 |website=Tate |archive-date=2022-08-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220830084944/https://www.tate.org.uk/art/archive/items/tga-8728-1-3-102/piper-photograph-of-chackmore-fountain-in-stowe-buckinghamshire |url-status=live }}</ref>

;The Forecourt
[[File:Stowe gardens book Nelsons Seat.jpg|thumb|upright|Nelson's Seat]]
* Nelson's Seat. This feature was located near to the north-west of the house, and was built around 1719–1720 to a design by Vanbrugh. It was named after William Nelson, the foreman in-charge of building it. It was remodelled in 1773 with a Doric portico added and demolished before 1797. The site is now marked by a grass mound.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=135}}

;The Western Garden
* The Queen's Theatre. Built in 1721, stretching from the ''Rotondo'' to the south vista. This consisted of a formal canal basin and elaborate grass terracing, which was re-landscaped around 1762–1764 to match the naturalistic form of the gardens as a whole.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=136}}

[[File:Stowe347718.jpg|right|thumb|The Vanbrugh Pyramid]]
* The Vanbrugh Pyramid. This was situated in the north-western corner of the garden. Erected in 1726 to a design by Vanbrugh, it was {{cvt|60|ft|m}} in height of steeply stepped form. It was demolished in 1797 and only the foundations survive today.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=137}} The pyramid carried this inscription by [[Gilbert West]]:
{{poemquote|Lamented Vanbrugh! This thy last Design,
Among the various Structures, that around,
Form'd by thy Hand, adorn this happy Ground,
This, sacred to thy Memory shall stand:
Cobham, and grateful Friendship so command.}}
* St. Augustine's Cave. A rustic edifice with a thatched roof that was built in the 1740s but had disappeared by 1797.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=139}}

[[File:Stowe gardens book Bacchus.jpg|thumb|upright|The Temple of Bacchus]]
* The Temple of Bacchus. This was designed by Vanbrugh and built in 1718, to the west of the house. It originally was built of brick but was later covered in [[stucco]] and further embellished with two lead [[sphinxes]]. It was demolished in 1926 to make way for the large school chapel designed by Sir [[Robert Lorimer]].{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=134}}
* Coucher's Obelisk. A dwarf obelisk erected before 1725, which was subsequently moved at least twice to other locations in the garden until its removal in 1763. It commemorated Reverend Robert Coucher, chaplain to Lord Cobham's dragoons.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=135}}
* Cowper's Urn. A large stone urn surrounded by a wooden seat. It was erected in 1827 just to the west of the ''Hermitage'', and sold in 1921. Its current location is unknown.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=146}}

[[File:Sleeping Parlour site - Stowe Gardens - Buckinghamshire, England - DSC06997.jpg|right|thumb|Foundations of the Sleeping Parlour]]
* The Queen of Hanover's Seat. Located in a clearing south-west of the site of the Temple of Bacchus. Originally called the Saxon Altar, it was the focus of the circle of ''Saxon Deities'' in 1727. It was moved in 1744 to the Grecian Valley to serve as a base of a statue of a 'Dancing Faun' until being moved to this location in 1843 and inscribed to commemorate a visit by the Queen of [[Hanover]] in that year. Sold in 1921, it is now found in a garden in [[Yorkshire]].{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=148}}
* The Sleeping Parlour. Probably designed by Vanbrugh, this was erected in 1725 in the woods next to the South Vista. It was square with Ionic porticoes on two sides, one inscribed ''Omnia sint in incerto, fave tibi'' (Since all things are uncertain, indulge thyself). It was demolished in 1760.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=138}}
* The Cold Bath. Built around 1723 to a design by Vanbrugh, it was a simple brick structure located near the Cascade. Demolished by 1761.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=137}}

;The Elysian Fields
* The Temple of Modern Virtue. Once found to the south of the Temple of Ancient Virtue. It was built in 1737 as an ironic classical ruin, with a headless statue in contemporary dress. It was left to collapse to ruin and today there are remnants found in the undergrowth.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=139}}
* The Gosfield Altar. Located on an island in the lake, this was an Antique classical altar erected by [[Louis XVIII]] of France in gratitude for being allowed to use [[Gosfield Hall]] in Essex. It was moved from there by the 1st Duke in 1825. It had disappeared by 1843.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MNA131246: National Trust Heritage Records |url=https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA131246 |access-date=2022-08-30 |website=heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk |archive-date=2022-08-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220830084948/https://heritagerecords.nationaltrust.org.uk/HBSMR/MonRecord.aspx?uid=MNA131246 |url-status=live }}</ref>
* The Temple of Contemplation. Since replaced by the Four Seasons Fountain. It was in existence by 1750 and had a simple arcaded front with pediment. It was later used as a cold bath until replaced by the fountain.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=139}}
* The Witch House. It was built in 1738 and was located in a clearing behind the ''Temple of Ancient Virtue''. It was built of brick with sloping walls and a heavy, over-sailing roof, the interior had a mural painting of a [[Witchcraft|witch]]. It is unknown when it was demolished.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=139}}
* The 1st Duchess's Urn. Once located near the Gothic Cross. It was made of white marble and erected by the 2nd Duke to commemorate his mother.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=148}}

;The Eastern Garden
* The Imperial Closet. This small building was situated to the east of the ''Temple of Friendship'' designed by Gibbs and built in 1739. The interior had paintings of [[Titus]], [[Hadrian]] and [[Marcus Aurelius]] with these inscriptions beneath each painting: ''{{lang|la|Diem perdidi}}'' ("I have lost the day"); ''{{lang|la|Pro me: si merear in me}}'' ("For me, but if I deserve it, against me"); and ''{{lang|la|Ita regnes imperator, ut privatus regi te velis}}'' ("So govern when an emperor, as, if a private person, you would desire to be governed"). The building was demolished in 1759.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=141}}
* The 1st Duke's Urn. Erected in 1841 by the 2nd Duke to commemorate his father. It stood near the path to the Lamport Gardens. It was removed in 1931 to the school.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=148}}

;The Grecian Valley
* Sculpture: the valley used to have several lead sculptures placed at strategic points around it, including '[[Hercules]] and [[Antaeus]]', '[[Cain and Abel]]', 'Hercules and the Boar', 'The Athlete' and 'The Dancing Faun'.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|pp=144-146}}

Several of the sculptures are located at [[Trent Park]], purchased by [[Philip Sassoon]] in 1921. They include:

