== Early tourism ==
[[File:A PLAN OF THE MAGNIFICENT STOWE HOUSE, BUCKINGHAMSHIRE.jpg|thumb|upright|A Plan of the Magnificent Stowe House, Buckinghamshire - Benton Seeley, 1766 (Garden Museum)]]

The New Inn public house was constructed in 1717, and provided lodging and food for visitors who had come to admire the gardens and the park, with its neo-classical sculptures and buildings.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The New Inn at Stowe |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/the-new-inn-at-stowe |access-date=2022-07-15 |website=National Trust |archive-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715072007/https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/stowe/features/the-new-inn-at-stowe |url-status=live }}</ref> During the eighteenth century, visitors arrived at the Bell Gate.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=10}}

Stowe was the subject of some of the earliest tourist guide books published in Britain, written to guide visitors around the site.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=10}} The first was published in 1744 by [[Benton Seeley]], founder of [[Seeley, Service]], who produced ''A Description of the Gardens of Lord Cobham at Stow Buckinghamshire''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Anderson |first=Jocelyn |title=When were country house guidebooks invented? |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/features/when-were-country-house-guidebooks-invented |access-date=2022-07-15 |website=National Trust |archive-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715091301/https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/features/when-were-country-house-guidebooks-invented |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=A plan of the magnificent Stowe House, Buckinghamshire - Benton Seeley |url=https://artsandculture.google.com/asset/a-plan-of-the-magnificent-stowe-house-buckinghamshire-benton-seeley/YwEQDQngJn7t6A |access-date=2022-07-15 |website=Google Arts & Culture |archive-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715091301/https://artsandculture.google.com/asset/a-plan-of-the-magnificent-stowe-house-buckinghamshire-benton-seeley/YwEQDQngJn7t6A |url-status=live }}</ref> The final edition of this series was published in 1838.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|p=10}}

In 1748 [[William Gilpin (clergyman)|William Gilpin]] produced ''Views of the Temples and other Ornamental Buildings in the Gardens at Stow'', followed in 1749 by ''A Dialogue upon the Gardens at Stow''.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Losev |first1=Lev |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=k1lgAAAAMAAJ&q=Views+of+the+Temples+and+other+Ornamental+Buildings+in+the+Gardens+at+Stow |title=A Sense of Place: Tsarskoe Selo and Its Poets : Papers from the 1989 Dartmouth Conference Dedicated to the Centennial of Anna Akhmatova |last2=Scherr |first2=Barry P. |date=1993 |publisher=Slavica Publishers |isbn=978-0-89357-239-6 |pages=22 |access-date=30 January 2023 |archive-date=9 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230309015356/https://books.google.com/books?id=k1lgAAAAMAAJ&q=Views+of+the+Temples+and+other+Ornamental+Buildings+in+the+Gardens+at+Stow |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Gilpin |first=William |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=U6Q3DwAAQBAJ&dq=The+Gardens+of+the+Right+Honourable+Richard+Viscount+Cobham&pg=PT2 |title=A Dialogue upon the Gardens of the Right Honorouble the Lord Viscount Cobham at Stow in Buckinghamshire |date=2021-12-02 |publisher=Litres |isbn=978-5-04-082839-5 |access-date=2022-07-27 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044918/https://books.google.com/books?id=U6Q3DwAAQBAJ&dq=The+Gardens+of+the+Right+Honourable+Richard+Viscount+Cobham&pg=PT2 |url-status=live }}</ref> In Gilpin's ''Dialogue'' two mythical figures, Callophilus and Polypthon, prefer different styles of gardening at Stowe to each other: Callophilus prefers formality; Polypthon, the romantic and ruinous.{{sfn|Bevington|1994|pp=53-4}}

Copies of all three books were published in 1750 by [[George Bickham the Elder]] as ''The Beauties of Stow''.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Balderston |first=Theodore |date=1992 |title=George R. Boyer. An Economic History of the English Poor Law, 1750–1850. New York: Cambridge University Press. 1990. Pp. xiii, 297. $44.50. |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/albion/article/abs/george-r-boyer-an-economic-history-of-the-english-poor-law-17501850-new-york-cambridge-university-press-1990-pp-xiii-297-4450/9911C11C0216717A89CE0875EAA3D4A2 |journal=Albion |volume=24 |issue=1 |pages=128–130 |doi=10.2307/4051268 |issn=0095-1390 |jstor=4051268 |access-date=2022-07-15 |archive-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715091301/https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/albion/article/abs/george-r-boyer-an-economic-history-of-the-english-poor-law-17501850-new-york-cambridge-university-press-1990-pp-xiii-297-4450/9911C11C0216717A89CE0875EAA3D4A2 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref>

To cater to the large number of visitors from France, an anonymous French guidebook, ''Les Charmes de Stow'', was published in 1748.<ref>{{Citation |last1=Châtel |first1=Laurent |title=Brown Invisible in France? The French Perception and Reception of Eighteenth-Century British Gardens |date=2020 |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctv1c9hmr8.17 |work=Capability Brown, Royal Gardener |pages=181–198 |editor-last=Finch |editor-first=Jonathan |series=The Business of Place-Making in Northern Europe |publisher=White Rose University Press |doi=10.2307/j.ctv1c9hmr8.17 |jstor=j.ctv1c9hmr8.17 |access-date=2022-07-15 |last2=Mosser |first2=Monique |editor2-last=Woudstra |editor2-first=Jan |s2cid=234647587 |archive-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715091302/https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctv1c9hmr8.17 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref> In the 1750s [[Jean-Jacques Rousseau]] wrote about the gardens, which spread their notoriety throughout Europe.{{sfn|Robinson|1990|p=111}} He had this to say:

{{Blockquote|text=Stowe is composed of very beautiful and very picturesque spots chosen to represent different kinds of scenery, all of which seem natural except when considered as a whole, as in the Chinese gardens of which I was telling you. The master and creator of this superb domain has also erected ruins, temples and ancient buildings, like the scenes, exhibit a magnificence which is more than human.|author=Jean Jacques Rousseau}}

Another francophone guide was published by {{ill|Georges-Louis Le Rouge|fr|Georges-Louis Le Rouge}} in 1777. ''Détails de nouveaux jardins à la mode'' included engravings of buildings at Stowe as well as at other famous gardens in Britain.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Le Rouge |first1=Georges-Louis |url=http://archive.org/details/gri_33125008481703 |title=Detail des nouveaux jardins à la mode |last2=Chambers |first2=William |last3=Richard |first3=Claude |last4=Thiemé |first4=Jardinier decorateur |date=1776 |publisher=A Paris : Chez Le Rouge |others=Getty Research Institute}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Curl |first1=James Stevens |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=4Lu6BwAAQBAJ&dq=D%C3%A9tails+de+nouveaux+jardins+%C3%A0+la+mode+stowe&pg=PA425 |title=The Oxford Dictionary of Architecture |last2=Wilson |first2=Susan |date=2015 |publisher=Oxford University Press |isbn=978-0-19-967498-5 |page=425 |access-date=2022-07-27 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044918/https://books.google.com/books?id=4Lu6BwAAQBAJ&dq=D%C3%A9tails+de+nouveaux+jardins+%C3%A0+la+mode+stowe&pg=PA425 |url-status=live }}</ref> In Germany, [[Christian Cay Lorenz Hirschfeld]] published ''Theorie der Gartenkunst'' in 5 volumes in [[Leipzig]] 1779–1785, which included Stowe.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Hirschfeld |first=Christian-Cajus-Lorenz |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=lqlSAAAAcAAJ&dq=Theorie+der+Gartenkunst+stowe&pg=PA61 |title=Theorie der Gartenkunst |date=1780 |publisher=Weidmanns Erben |page=61 |language=de |access-date=2022-07-27 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044919/https://books.google.com/books?id=lqlSAAAAcAAJ&dq=Theorie+der+Gartenkunst+stowe&pg=PA61 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Nekula |first=Marek |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=gnVPDgAAQBAJ&dq=Christian+Cay+Lorenz+Hirschfeld+stowe&pg=PA155 |title=Tod und Auferstehung einer Nation: Der Traum vom Pantheon in der tschechischen Literatur und Kultur |date=2017-03-13 |publisher=Böhlau Verlag Köln Weimar |isbn=978-3-412-22396-0 |page=155 |language=de |access-date=2022-07-27 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044945/https://books.google.com/books?id=gnVPDgAAQBAJ&dq=Christian+Cay+Lorenz+Hirschfeld+stowe&pg=PA155 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Cultural significance==

The [[World Monuments Fund]] describes Stowe as "one of the most beautiful and complex historic landscapes in Britain".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://wmf.org.uk/Projects/stowe-house/|title=Stowe House|publisher=[[World Monuments Fund]]|access-date=21 January 2023|archive-date=21 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121151315/https://wmf.org.uk/Projects/stowe-house/|url-status=live}}</ref> The range and stature of the designers deployed, including Bridgeman, Brown, Vanbrugh, Gibbs and Kent;<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wmf.org/project/stowe-house|title=Stowe|publisher=[[World Monuments Fund]]|access-date=21 January 2023|archive-date=21 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121151035/https://www.wmf.org/project/stowe-house|url-status=live}}</ref> the intricacy of the architectural and allegorical schemes those designers devised; the unified conception they created; the extent of its survival;{{sfn|Williamson|Pevsner|2003|p=660}} and its influence as the "birthplace of the English art of landscape gardening",{{sfn|Williamson|Pevsner|2003|p=660}} combine to make Stowe "a garden of international repute".{{sfn|Morris|2018|p=13}} Its importance is recognised in the large number of [[Listed buildings|listed structures]] within the garden and the wider park, and its own Grade I listing designation.<ref>{{NHLE|num=1000198|desc=Stowe Park and Garden|grade=I|access-date=21 January 2023}}</ref>

===Architecture and Horticulture===

The Temples' wealth and prestige enabled them to engage with the leading designers of the Georgian era. The outline of the present gardens was laid by Charles Bridgeman, and some of the earliest of the forty monuments and temples situated on the estate were designed by John Vanbrugh.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.royal-oak.org/past-projects/stowe-restoration-appeal/|title=Stowe Restoration Appeal|publisher=Royal Oak Society|access-date=22 January 2023|archive-date=21 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121185423/https://www.royal-oak.org/past-projects/stowe-restoration-appeal/|url-status=live}}</ref> They were followed by William Kent, James Gibbs and then by Capability Brown, who was appointed head gardener at Stowe at the age of 25, and later married in the estate church.{{sfn|Taylor|2008|pp=104-106}} Tim Knox, in his chapter "The Fame of Stowe", published in the Trust's book, ''Stowe Landscape Gardens'', suggests that Brown's subsequent career, which saw him deploy the expertise gained at Stowe across a large number of other landscape parks throughout England, may in fact be the garden's most significant legacy.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=90}} In addition to the major British architects deployed, the Temples engaged a number of prominent Europeans. Although they worked primarily on the house, they also contributed to some of the garden structures. [[Giovanni Battista Borra]] worked on the Temple of Concord and Victory and modernised the Boycott Pavilions and the Oxford Gate.{{sfn|Colvin|1978|p=127}} [[Jacques-François Blondel|Georges-François Blondel]] may have undertaken work on the Queen's Temple,{{sfn|Colvin|1978|p=114}} while [[Vincenzo Valdrè]] designed the Oxford Gate lodges, the base of the Cobham Monument and may have been responsible for the Menagerie.{{sfn|Colvin|1978|p=848}}

The work of so many major architects gives the gardens and park at Stowe a unique architectural flavour.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=88}} It is less a horticultural garden and more of a picturesque landscape of lawns, water and trees, with carefully contrived vistas which culminate in eye catching structures.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=4}}{{sfn|Williamson|Pevsner|2003|p=672}} Other gardens of the period, such as [[Claremont Landscape Garden|Claremont]], [[Kew Gardens|Kew]] and [[Stourhead]] followed this style, but few matched the scale of Stowe. While the buildings in the grounds at Stowe are natural foci for attention, the landscaping around the structures is as vital to the overall scheme.{{sfn|Harbison|2000|p=16}} The gardens progressed from a formal, structured layout, through increasing naturalisation.<ref name=":02">{{Cite book |last=Christie |first=Christopher |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=G-wOz1kFCdMC&q=%22planting+scheme%22+stowe |title=The British Country House in the Eighteenth Century |date=2000 |publisher=Manchester University Press |isbn=978-0-7190-4725-1 |pages=130–131, 146, 197 |access-date=23 October 2022 |archive-date=9 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230309015425/https://books.google.com/books?id=G-wOz1kFCdMC&q=%22planting+scheme%22+stowe |url-status=live }}</ref> The planting of grasses and trees was equally deliberate, designed to lead the eyes of the visitor on to the next area, and to bring a sense of drama to the landscape.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Starkey |first=Janet |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=zntTDwAAQBAJ&dq=trees+stowe+gardens&pg=PA304 |title=The Scottish Enlightenment Abroad: The Russells of Braidshaw in Aleppo and on the Coast of Coromandel |date=2018-03-20 |publisher=BRILL |isbn=978-90-04-36213-0 |page=304 |access-date=18 December 2022 |archive-date=22 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230122075915/https://books.google.com/books?id=zntTDwAAQBAJ&dq=trees+stowe+gardens&pg=PA304 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The gardens incorporate a number of architectural and horticultural "firsts". They are themselves considered the earliest example of the English landscape garden.{{sfn|Williamson|Pevsner|2003|p=660}}<ref>{{cite web|url=https://buckinghamshire-gov-uk.s3.amazonaws.com/documents/Stowe-CA.pdf|title=Stowe Conservation Area|publisher=Buckinghamshire County Council|date=December 2008|access-date=22 January 2023|archive-date=21 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121193504/https://buckinghamshire-gov-uk.s3.amazonaws.com/documents/Stowe-CA.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref> Defining the borders of the park he began, Charles Bridgeman designed the first [[ha-ha]] in England, a feature that was widely imitated.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://wmf.org.uk/Projects/history-of-the-house/|title=Stowe - History|publisher=[[World Monuments Fund]]|access-date=22 January 2023|archive-date=21 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121222219/https://wmf.org.uk/Projects/history-of-the-house/|url-status=live}}</ref> Within the garden, Kent's Chinese House was perhaps England's earliest ''[[Chinoiserie]]'' building.{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=45}} So notable were the gardens at Stowe that they were emulated across the world. [[Thomas Jefferson]] visited, and bought the guidebooks, transporting ideas across the Atlantic for his [[Monticello]] estate.<ref name=":32"/> Eastwards, it inspired gardens in Germany such as that at [[Dessau-Wörlitz Garden Realm|Wörlitz]],<ref name=":0" /> and those created at [[Peterhof Palace|Peterhof]] and [[Tsarskoye Selo]] by [[Catherine the Great]].{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|pp=27-28}}{{efn|The profusion of buildings in the grounds did not meet with universal approval. [[Hermann, Fürst von Pückler-Muskau]], the aristocratic tourist who visited Stowe in 1818, remarked; "it is so overcrowded with temples of all kinds that the greatest improvement that could be carried out here would consist in pulling down about ten or twelve of them."{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=79}}}}

===Sermon in Stone – the "meaning" of the Garden===

A central element of the uniqueness of Stowe were the efforts of its owners to tell a story within, and through, the landscape. A symposium organised by the [[Courtauld Institute]], ''The Garden at War: Deception, Craft and Reason'', suggests that it was not "a garden of flowers or shrubs [but] of ideas."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://courtauld.ac.uk/whats-on/courtauld-symposium-stowe-2017/|title=The Garden at War|publisher=Courtauld Institute|date=8 July 2017|access-date=22 January 2023|archive-date=21 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121193231/https://courtauld.ac.uk/whats-on/courtauld-symposium-stowe-2017/|url-status=live}}</ref> The original concept may have been derived from an essay written by [[Joseph Addison]] for the ''[[Tatler (1709 journal)|Tatler]]'' magazine.{{sfn|Dixon-Hunt|Willis|1998|p=138}} The landscape was to be a "sermon in stone",{{sfn|Clarke|Marsden|Wheeler|Bevington|1997|p=7}} emphasising the perceived Whig triumphs of Reason, the Enlightenment, [[Bill of Rights 1689|liberty]] and the [[Glorious Revolution]], and 'British' virtues of [[Protestantism]], [[British Empire|empire]], and curbs on [[Absolutism (European history)|absolutist monarchical power]]. These were to stand in contrast with the debased values of the corrupt political regime then prevailing.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://bucksgardenstrust.org.uk/unforgettable-gardens/unforgettable-gardens-the-elysian-fields-at-stowe-a-personal-view/|title=The Elysium Fields at Stowe|first=Geoff|last=Huntingford|publisher=Buckinghamshire Gardens Trust|date=9 May 2021|access-date=22 January 2023|archive-date=21 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121203712/https://bucksgardenstrust.org.uk/unforgettable-gardens/unforgettable-gardens-the-elysian-fields-at-stowe-a-personal-view/|url-status=live}}</ref> The temples of Ancient Virtues and British Worthies were material expressions of what the Temples themselves supported, while the intentionally ruined Temple of Modern Virtue was a contemptuous depiction of what they opposed, the buildings and their setting making a clear moral and political statement.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://press.uchicago.edu/Misc/Chicago/728226.html|title=What Gardens Mean: Stowe|first=Stephanie|last=Ross|publisher=University of Chicago Press|date=1998|access-date=22 January 2023|archive-date=6 October 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221006125328/https://press.uchicago.edu/Misc/Chicago/728226.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Praising the "grandeur of [its] overall conception", [[John Julius Norwich]] considered that the garden at Stowe better expressed the beliefs and values of its creators, the [[Patriot Whigs|Whig Aristocracy]], "than any other house in England."{{sfn|Norwich|1985|p=69}}

As Stowe evolved from an English baroque garden into a pioneering landscape park, the gardens became an attraction for many of the nobility, including political leaders.<ref name=":22">{{Cite book |last=Impelluso |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=NRYqPzukxFcC&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PA96 |title=Gardens in Art |date=2007 |publisher=Getty Publications |isbn=978-0-89236-885-3 |pages=96–98 |access-date=23 October 2022 |archive-date=9 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230309015350/https://books.google.com/books?id=NRYqPzukxFcC&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PA96 |url-status=live }}</ref> Many of the temples and monuments in the garden celebrate the political ideas of the Whig party.<ref name=":32">{{Cite book |last=Webster |first=Sally |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=GCQxDwAAQBAJ&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PT47 |title=The Nation's First Monument and the Origins of the American Memorial Tradition: Liberty Enshrined |date=2017-07-05 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-351-54201-2 |access-date=23 October 2022 |archive-date=22 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230122075914/https://books.google.com/books?id=GCQxDwAAQBAJ&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PT47 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Fitzpatrick |first1=Martin |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=SyzL0nHRM8UC&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PA291 |title=Enlightenment World |last2=Jones |first2=Peter |last3=Knellwolf |first3=Christa |last4=McCalman |first4=Iain |date=2004-07-22 |publisher=Psychology Press |isbn=978-0-415-21575-6 |page=291 |access-date=23 October 2022 |archive-date=9 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230309015346/https://books.google.com/books?id=SyzL0nHRM8UC&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PA291 |url-status=live }}</ref> They also include quotes by many of the writers who are part of Augustan literature, also philosophers and ideas belonging to the [[Age of Enlightenment]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Delon |first=Michel |chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=QEpJAgAAQBAJ&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PA572 |title=Encyclopedia of the Enlightenment |date=2013-12-04 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-135-95998-2 |page=855<!--572--> |chapter=Monument |access-date=23 October 2022 |archive-date=9 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230309015425/https://books.google.com/books?id=QEpJAgAAQBAJ&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PA572 |url-status=live }}</ref> The Temple family used the construction of the Temple of Ancient Virtue, modelled on the [[Temple of the Sibyl|Temple of Sibyl]] in [[Tivoli, Lazio|Tivoli]], to assert their place as a family of 'ancient virtue'.<ref name=":22" /><ref name=":02" /> Figures depicted in the temple include [[Homer]], [[Socrates]], [[Epaminondas]] and [[Lycurgus (lawgiver)|Lycurgus]], whose attributes are described with Latin inscriptions that promote them as "defenders of liberty".<ref name=":32" />

Richard Temple was also the leader of a political faction known as [[Cobhamites|Cobham's Cubs]], established as opposition to the policies of [[Robert Walpole]].<ref name=":13">{{Cite ODNB |title=Cobham's Cubs (act. 1734–1747) |url=https://www.oxforddnb.com/view/10.1093/ref:odnb/9780198614128.001.0001/odnb-9780198614128-e-93706 |access-date=2022-10-09 | year=2004 |doi=10.1093/ref:odnb/93706| isbn=978-0-19-861412-8 }}</ref> Part of the gardens at Stowe were altered to illustrate this rivalry: Temple erected the Temple of Modern Virtue, purposefully constructed as a ruin and located next to a decaying statue of Walpole.<ref name=":13" /> (The Temple of Modern Virtue is no longer extant.<ref name="Hunt 1971 294–317">{{Cite journal |last=Hunt |first=John Dixon |date=1971 |title=Emblem and Expressionism in the Eighteenth-Century Landscape Garden |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/2737734 |journal=Eighteenth-Century Studies |volume=4 |issue=3 |pages=294–317 |doi=10.2307/2737734 |jstor=2737734 |access-date=9 October 2022 |archive-date=9 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221009195435/https://www.jstor.org/stable/2737734 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref>)

The principles of the English landscape garden were unpopular with Tory supporters who, according to the historian Christopher Christie, did not approve of how they "displayed in a very conspicuous way" the estate and parkland. There was also concern, from commentators such as [[Oliver Goldsmith]], that demolishing the homes of tenants was "unacceptable and an abuse of power".<ref name=":02" />

Contemporary satire reflected the role the gardens played in political life by portraying caricatures of the better-known politicians of history taking their ease in similar settings.<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Umbach |first=Maiken |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=EY_RC1RCuRIC&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PA32 |title=Federalism & Englightenment in Ger |date=2000-01-01 |publisher=A&C Black |isbn=978-1-85285-177-4 |page=35 |access-date=23 October 2022 |archive-date=9 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230309015410/https://books.google.com/books?id=EY_RC1RCuRIC&dq=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens&pg=PA32 |url-status=live }}</ref> In 1762, [[Henry Home, Lord Kames]], a philosopher, commented that for the visitor the political commentary within the garden at Stowe may be "something they guess" rather than clearly explained.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Richardson |first=Tim |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Zsfi7Pa6-oAC&q=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens |title=The Arcadian Friends |date=2011-11-30 |publisher=Transworld |isbn=978-1-4464-3653-0 |page=327 |access-date=23 October 2022 |archive-date=9 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230309015347/https://books.google.com/books?id=Zsfi7Pa6-oAC&q=enlightenment+politics+stowe+gardens |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Art ===

Charles Bridgeman commissioned 15 [[engraving]]s of the gardens from [[Jacques Rigaud]] ([[:fr:Jacques Rigaud (artist)|fr]]), which were published in 1739.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Willis |first=Peter |date=1972 |title=Jacques Rigaud's Drawings of Stowe in the Metropolitan Museum of Art |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/3031563 |journal=Eighteenth-Century Studies |volume=6 |issue=1 |pages=85–98 |doi=10.2307/3031563 |issn=0013-2586 |jstor=3031563 |access-date=2022-07-15 |archive-date=2022-07-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715091301/https://www.jstor.org/stable/3031563 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref> The etching was undertaken by another French artist, [[Bernard Baron]]. They show views of the gardens with an array of fashionable figures, including the Italian [[castrato]] [[Senesino]], disporting themselves in the foreground. One set is held in the [[Royal Collection]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rct.uk/collection/themes/exhibitions/painting-paradise-the-art-of-the-garden-the-queens-gallery-buckingh-10|title=Four views of the gardens at Stowe|publisher=Royal Collection Trust|access-date=23 January 2023|archive-date=23 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230123083004/https://www.rct.uk/collection/themes/exhibitions/painting-paradise-the-art-of-the-garden-the-queens-gallery-buckingh-10|url-status=live}}</ref> In 1805-9 [[John Claude Nattes]] painted 105 wash drawings of both the house and gardens.<ref>Aldred, John. "John Claude Nattes." ''British Art Journal'' 4.2 (2003).</ref> Stowe is one of the houses and gardens depicted on the [[Frog Service|frog service]], a dinner service for fifty people commissioned from [[Wedgwood]] by Catherine the Great for her palace at [[Tsarskoye Selo]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-07-21 |title=How Josiah Wedgwood's Frog Service depicted Britain in chinaware |url=https://www.newstatesman.com/culture/2021/07/how-josiah-wedgwood-s-frog-service-depicted-britain-chinaware |access-date=2022-08-04 |website=New Statesman |archive-date=2022-08-04 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220804172352/https://www.newstatesman.com/culture/2021/07/how-josiah-wedgwood-s-frog-service-depicted-britain-chinaware |url-status=live }}</ref> [[John Piper (artist)|John Piper]] produced watercolours of some of the monuments in the gardens, including the Temple of British Worthies, amongst others.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Victoria and Albert Museum |author-link=Victoria and Albert Museum |title=The Temple of British Worthies, Stowe, Gloucestershire {{!}} Piper, John: V&A Explore The Collections |url=https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O16304/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220805071817/https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O16304/ |archive-date=2022-08-05 |access-date=2022-08-05 |website=Victoria and Albert Museum: Explore the Collections|date=21 April 1940 }}</ref>

The gardens at Stowe were as much influenced ''by'' art as they provided an inspiration for it. The idealised [[pastoral]] landscapes of [[Claude Lorrain]] and [[Nicolas Poussin]], with their echoes of an earlier [[Arcadia (utopia)|Arcadia]], led English aristocrats with the necessary means to attempt to recreate the [[Roman Campagna]] on their English estates.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/gard_3/hd_gard_3.htm|title=From Geometric to Informal Gardens in the Eighteenth Century|first=Vanessa|last=Bezemer Sellers|publisher=[[Metropolitan Museum of Art]]|date=October 2003|access-date=22 January 2023|archive-date=21 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121221052/https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/gard_3/hd_gard_3.htm|url-status=live}}</ref> Kent's acquaintance, [[Joseph Spence (author)|Joseph Spence]], considered that his Elysian Fields were "a picture translated into a garden".{{sfn|Harris|1994|p=198}}

=== Poetry ===

[[Alexander Pope]] who first stayed at the house in 1724, celebrated the design of Stowe as part of a tribute to [[Richard Boyle, 3rd Earl of Burlington]].<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Rogers |first1=DeBartolo Professor in the Liberal Arts Pat |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=udRlsDhfhoUC&dq=alexander+pope+stowe+1724&pg=PA284 |title=The Alexander Pope Encyclopedia |last2=Rogers |first2=Pat |date=2004 |publisher=Greenwood Publishing Group |isbn=978-0-313-32426-0 |page=284 |access-date=2022-07-27 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044946/https://books.google.com/books?id=udRlsDhfhoUC&dq=alexander+pope+stowe+1724&pg=PA284 |url-status=live }}</ref> The full title of the 1st edition (1731) was ''An Epistle to the Right Honourable Richard Earl of Burlington, Occasion'd by his Publishing Palladio's Designs of the Baths, Arches, Theatres, &c. of Ancient Rome''.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Pope |first=Alexander |url=http://archive.org/details/gri_33125008438711 |title=An epistle to the Right Honourable Richard, Earl of Burlington : occasion'd by his publishing Palladio's designs of the baths, arches, theatres, &c. of ancient Rome |date=1731 |publisher=Printed for L. Gilliver |others=Digitisation sponsored by Getty Research Institute |location=London}}</ref> Lines 65–70 of the poem run:

{{poemquote|Still follow Sense, of ev'ry Art the Soul,
Parts answ'ring parts shall slide into a whole,
Spontaneous beauties all around advance,
Start ev'n from Difficulty, strike from Chance;
Nature shall join you, Time shall make it grow
A Work to wonder at—perhaps a STOWE.|title=An Epistle to the Right Honourable Richard Earl of Burlington, Occasion'd by his Publishing Palladio's Designs of the Baths, Arches, Theatres, &c. of Ancient Rome|char=|sign=Alexander Pope|source=}}

In 1730 [[James Thomson (poet, born 1700)|James Thomson]] published his poem ''Autumn'', part of his four works ''The Seasons.''<ref>{{Cite book |last=Terry |first=Richard |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2bO-JB0vAaQC&dq=james+thomson+stowe&pg=PA104 |title=James Thomson: Essays for the Tercentenary |date=2000-01-01 |publisher=Liverpool University Press |isbn=978-0-85323-964-2 |page=104 |access-date=2022-07-27 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044949/https://books.google.com/books?id=2bO-JB0vAaQC&dq=james+thomson+stowe&pg=PA104 |url-status=live }}</ref> Stowe is referenced in lines 1040–46:

{{poemquote|Or is this gloom too much? Then lead, ye powers
That o'er the garden and the rural seat
Preside, which shining through the cheerful land
In countless numbers blest Britannia sees,
Oh lead me to the wide-extended walks,
The fair majestic paradise of Stowe!|sign=James Thomson|title=Autumn}}

In 1732 Lord Cobham's nephew [[Gilbert West]] wrote a lengthy poem, ''The Gardens of the Right Honourable Richard Viscount Cobham'', a guide to the gardens in verse form.<ref>{{Cite book |last=West |first=Gilbert |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qUZgAAAAcAAJ&q=The+Gardens+of+the+Right+Honourable+Richard+Viscount+Cobham |title=Stowe, the gardens of the Right Honourable Richard Lord Viscount Cobham. Address'd to Mr. Pope. [By Gilbert West.] To which is added, Of False Taste. A poem. By Mr. Pope |date=1732 |publisher=George Faulkner |access-date=2022-07-27 |archive-date=2022-09-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220910044950/https://books.google.com/books?id=qUZgAAAAcAAJ&q=The+Gardens+of+the+Right+Honourable+Richard+Viscount+Cobham |url-status=live }}</ref> Another poem which included references to Stowe is ''The Enthusiast; or lover of nature'' by [[Joseph Warton]].<ref name="Hunt 1971 294–317"/>

=== Historic Importance ===

Stowe has a "more remarkable collection of garden buildings than any other park in [England]".{{sfn|Mowl|Earnshaw|1985|p=32}} Some forty structures remain in the garden and wider park; Elizabeth Williamson considered that the number of extant structures made Stowe unique.{{sfn|Williamson|Pevsner|2003|p=660}} Of these, some 27 separate garden buildings are designated [[Listed Building|Grade I]], [[Historic England]]'s highest grade, denoting buildings of "exceptional interest".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.culture.gov.uk/images/publications/Principles_Selection_Listing.pdf |title=Principles of Selection for Listing Buildings |publisher=Department of Culture, Media and Sport |format=.pdf |date=March 2010 |access-date=24 May 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=http://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/20121204113822/http://www.culture.gov.uk/images/publications/Principles_Selection_Listing.pdf |archive-date=4 December 2012 }}</ref> The remainder are listed at Grade II* or Grade II. The garden and surrounding park are themselves listed at Grade I on the [[Register of Historic Parks and Gardens of special historic interest in England|Register of Historic Parks and Gardens]].<ref name="NHLEGardens">{{NHLE|num=1000198|desc=Stowe|access-date=5 February 2016|mode=cs2}}</ref>

In the opening chapter of ''Stowe House: Saving an Architectural Masterpiece'', the most recent study of the house and the estate, [[Jeremy Musson]] describes the mansion as "the centrepiece of a landscape garden of international repute",{{sfn|Morris|2018|p=13}} while the National Trust, the garden's custodian, suggests that the estate is "one of the most remarkable legacies of [[Georgian era|Georgian]] England".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/oxfordshire-buckinghamshire-berkshire/stowe/history-of-stowe|title=History of Stowe|publisher=National Trust|access-date=22 January 2023|archive-date=21 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230121183321/https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/oxfordshire-buckinghamshire-berkshire/stowe/history-of-stowe|url-status=live}}</ref> The architectural historian [[Christopher Hussey (historian)|Christopher Hussey]] declared the garden at Stowe to be the "outstanding monument to English Landscape Gardening".<ref>{{cite journal|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/3817691|first=Richard|last=Wheeler|title=The Park and Garden Survey at Stowe: The Replanting and Restoration of the Historic Landscape|journal=Huntington Library Quarterly|date=1992|volume=55|issue=3|pages=527–532|doi=10.2307/3817691|jstor=3817691|access-date=22 January 2023|archive-date=22 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230122181754/https://www.jstor.org/stable/3817691|url-status=live|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

